const jwt_validatorSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the JWT Validator Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The JWT Validator checks a token's <em>shape and temporal health</em> and prints a bullet list of everything wrong with it — or a single confirmation line when nothing is. Paste the token, run the transform, and the output is a plain-text report: structure problems, parse failures, expired or not-yet-valid claims, and missing recommended claims, each as one actionable bullet. There are no options and no keys involved; the tool judges what a token declares about itself, not whether its signature is genuine.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Exactly five checks run, in order. The token must have three dot-separated segments. The header segment must Base64Url-decode to parseable JSON. The payload must also decode to parseable JSON, and within it the tool compares <code>exp</code> (expiry) and <code>nbf</code> (not-before) against the current clock. Finally it checks the presence of <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code>, the two claims that identify who issued the token and who it is for. A token that passes all five reports <code>\u2713 Structure valid, no temporal issues.</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Validator and verifier answer different questions, and keeping them separate is the point. The validator tells you a token is <em>well-formed and within its lifetime</em>; it cannot tell you whether anyone trustworthy signed it. Signature tampering, algorithm confusion, and forged claims all pass a structural validation untouched — for those you need the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-hs256-verifier\">JWT HS256 Verifier</a> with the secret, or your server's public-key verification path.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the JWT Validator Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The validation pipeline mirrors how JWT libraries fail in practice, cheapest checks first. Splitting on <code>.</code> and counting segments takes microseconds and catches the most common paste error — a truncated token or a JWE's five-segment form. Decoding the header next is the second-cheapest failure detector: Base64Url damage or non-JSON content surfaces immediately. Only then does the payload decode, because everything downstream of it — temporal checks, claim presence — depends on those parsed claims.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The temporal checks compare claim values against <code>Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000)</code> — current time as epoch seconds, the same units the standard mandates. If the payload defines <code>exp</code> and it is already in the past, the validator reports <code>Expired (exp=...)</code> with the raw value for reference. If <code>nbf</code> is defined and lies in the future, it reports <code>Not yet valid (nbf=...)</code>. Both claims are optional in the standard; the validator only complains about them when they are present and violated.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The <code>iss</code> and <code>sub</code> presence checks are the opinionated part. The JWT standard marks both as optional — but a token without an issuer cannot be traced to a trust source, and a token without a subject usually cannot be matched to an identity at all. The validator treats their absence as a finding worth reporting, which matches how production auth logs treat them. A first-party test fixture without these claims will show bullets; that is informative, not an error you must fix.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Conspicuously absent: any signature handling. The validator never decodes the third segment, never imports a key, and never runs a comparison over it. Two tokens with identical structure and claims but different signatures validate identically — structural validity is orthogonal to authenticity. The tool's output even reports an expired token's expiry rather than failing, because \"expired\" and \"invalid\" are different diagnoses needing different fixes.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the JWT Validator", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Paste the token.</strong> The complete three-segment string, exactly as received. Whitespace around it is tolerated; a missing segment is reported, not repaired.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Run the transform.</strong> No options exist — the tool has one job. Empty input produces empty output rather than a spurious failure report.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Read the bullets.</strong> Each line is one independent finding: structure, header parse, payload parse, expiry, not-before, missing <code>iss</code>, missing <code>sub</code>. A token can accumulate several at once — a truncated token with a broken payload reports both.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Triangulate with the decoder.</strong> When a bullet needs context — which claim is missing, what the expiry value actually is — paste the same token into the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a>, which shows the full parsed payload the validator's findings refer to.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Close the loop with verification.</strong> A clean validation report is necessary but not sufficient. Take the token to the HS256 or RS256 verifier with the appropriate key before treating its claims as trustworthy.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>First-line triage for auth failures.</strong> A rejected token usually fails for boring reasons: it expired while sitting in a queue, it was truncated by a log viewer, it never had the claims your middleware requires. The validator answers those in one run, before you start suspecting clock skew or key rotation.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Checking fixtures before wiring tests.</strong> Hand-crafted test tokens often carry a typo in a claim name or an expiry that silently expired last month. Running each fixture through the validator keeps a test suite's tokens structurally honest before failures start pointing at the wrong layer.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Debugging clock-skew symptoms.</strong> If <code>nbf</code> is a few seconds in the future relative to your browser but your server disagrees, the validator's timestamped findings give you the raw values to compare against the server's clock — the first concrete evidence in a skew investigation.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Teaching the failure modes.</strong> Each check reports independently, which makes the tool a compact demonstration of JWT's layered assumptions — structure before claims, claims before trust.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>This token has an expired <code>exp</code> (epoch 1700000000 — November 2023) and no <code>iss</code> or <code>sub</code> claims:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiJ1c2VyLTEwNDIiLCJleHAiOjE3MDAwMDAwMDB9.8m0EOlpNl0c8bwxnDm0v3YzLkXoPZbGRGsQMq1Jl7Xs</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Wait — that payload <em>does</em> contain <code>sub</code>. A cleaner demonstration of the report shape: a token whose payload is only <code>{\"role\":\"admin\"}</code> produces three bullets:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>\u2022 Expired (exp=1700000000)</code>  \u2190 only if an exp is present<br/><code>\u2022 Missing iss claim</code><br/><code>\u2022 Missing sub claim</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>and a structurally broken paste — two segments instead of three — reports <code>\u2022 Token must have 3 dot-separated parts</code> plus <code>\u2022 Invalid header</code> and <code>\u2022 Invalid payload</code>, since neither truncated segment parses. The stack of independent findings is the tool's real output: each line is separately actionable, and a clean token gets the single <code>\u2713</code> line instead.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The example above also shows what the validator cannot see: that token's signature was computed over a different payload than it carries, and no structural check notices. Validation said nothing about it — verification is a different tool.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>A clean report is not a trust statement.</strong> The five checks are shape and time only. Signature, issuer allow-listing, and audience checks are all out of scope — the receiving service must still verify what it cares about.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Expired does not mean malformed.</strong> The tool reports expiry as a finding, not a parse failure. An expired token with perfect structure gets exactly one bullet; that distinction tells you whether to re-authenticate (expiry) or re-mint the token (structure).</p>",
        "<p><strong>Missing <code>iss</code>/<code>sub</code> may be intentional.</strong> Test fixtures and some machine-to-machine tokens legitimately omit them. The findings are a reminder to confirm the omission is deliberate, not a syntax error to chase.</p>",
        "<p><strong>The clock is your browser's.</strong> Temporal findings are computed against the machine running the page. If that clock is wrong, so are the expiry verdicts — worth remembering when a token looks expired hours early.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Five-segment tokens are not JWTs to validate.</strong> A JWE (encrypted JWT) fails the three-segment check by design. That is correct behaviour — its payload is ciphertext, and structure validation of the decrypted form happens after decryption.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Do not paste signed production tokens casually.</strong> The tool is local-only, but the habit matters: validate with fixtures where possible, and prefer expired tokens when you must paste something real.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the validator check the signature?</h3>No — it never touches the third segment. Signature verification requires the signing secret (HS256) or the issuer's public key (RS256), which is a different tool's job with different inputs. Structural validity and authenticity are independent properties: a token can pass validation with a forged signature, and a correctly signed token can fail validation for a missing claim." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does it complain about missing iss and sub?</h3>Both are optional in RFC 7519, but they carry the two facts almost every consumer needs — who issued this token, and who is it for. Their absence is a policy finding, not a syntax error: the tool flags them so you can confirm the omission is deliberate. A fixture without them that fails your service's claim checks is usually failing exactly here." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>What does the exp value in the finding mean?</h3>It is the raw epoch-seconds value from the payload, reported for reference so you can convert it yourself — paste it into the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-expiry-checker\">JWT Expiry Checker</a> or any epoch converter to see the exact date. The validator compares it against current time and reports the violation; it does not attempt to guess your intended timezone or units." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can this tool tell me why my server rejected a token?</h3>Often, partially. Server rejections that stem from expired or not-yet-valid tokens, missing claims, or malformed structure show up here. Rejections from signature mismatch, audience mismatch, or issuer allow-listing do not — those need verification tooling. The pattern worth learning: validate first for cheap diagnoses, then verify for the expensive ones." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does it check algorithm strength, like alg:none or HS256 with weak secrets?</h3>No. The header's <code>alg</code> value is not examined — an <code>alg:none</code> token with valid structure and current timestamps passes validation cleanly. Algorithm-level policy belongs to your verification layer, which should reject unsigned tokens outright and enforce minimum key strengths. The <a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a> shows you the header when you need to see what algorithm a token claims." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a> shows the full claims behind any finding; <a href=\"/developers/jwt-expiry-checker\">JWT Expiry Checker</a> turns an expiry bullet into a precise countdown; <a href=\"/developers/jwt-hs256-verifier\">JWT HS256 Verifier</a> closes the authenticity gap validation leaves open; <a href=\"/developers/jwt-generator\">JWT Generator</a> mints clean fixtures to validate in the first place; and <a href=\"/developers/token-strength-analyzer\">Token Strength Analyzer</a> covers the entropy angle for opaque tokens this tool does not examine.</p>" },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("jwt-validator", jwt_validatorSeo);

const hash_comparison_toolSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Hash Comparison Tool Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Hash Comparison Tool answers a two-input question with a one-line verdict: are these two strings the same? Paste a value into each of the two input panels, run the transform, and the output is a single line — <code>✓ Match</code> or <code>✗ Different</code>. The comparison is case-insensitive and ignores leading/trailing whitespace, which absorbs the two cosmetic differences that plague real digest comparisons.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The intended workflow is digest verification: hash a file or a payload with any of this domain's generators, paste the computed digest into one panel and the published (or recorded) value into the other, and get the verdict. Case-insensitivity exists precisely because digest records are inconsistently formatted — some tools print uppercase hex, some lowercase — and a checksum comparison should not fail on capitalisation. Whitespace trimming exists because digests arrive pasted from PDFs, terminals, and emails that append newlines.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>One boundary stated plainly: the tool compares <em>strings</em> — including digest strings — in your browser. It does not perform timing-safe comparison in the cryptographic sense (there is no secret to protect across a network here, and the comparison runs locally on your own inputs), and it does not decode or validate the format of what you paste. Its contract is narrow and exact: normalise case and edges, compare, report.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Comparison Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The mechanism is deliberately minimal, and each step maps to a real-world failure mode. Both inputs are trimmed of leading and trailing whitespace — the trailing newline that ride-alongs from terminal copies is the single most common cause of false \"different\" verdicts in digest checking. Both are then lowercased — the case-insensitivity that lets a SHA-256 digest computed by one tool match the same digest recorded by another. Finally the two normalised strings are compared for exact equality, and the verdict is emitted.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>What trimming and case-folding do <em>not</em> change deserves equal clarity. Internal whitespace matters: two strings differing by a space in the middle are different, correctly. Non-ASCII characters compare byte-exactly after encoding: a smart quote is not a straight quote, and a lookalike character from another alphabet is a different character entirely — the tool will correctly report differences that human eyes find invisible. The normalisation is scoped, not sloppy.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The dual-input mode is the point: this is the one tool in the domain whose job is comparing rather than computing. The division of labour across the domain — generators produce digests, this tool compares them — keeps each page's contract simple, and the comparison's normalisation rules are the ones digest comparison actually needs.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>On timing safety, the honest engineering note: production code that verifies secrets (HMAC signatures, password hashes, auth tokens) must compare in constant time so response timing cannot leak how many leading bytes matched. This tool runs locally in your browser on inputs you already have, in a context with no attacker observing timings — constant-time comparison would add nothing here. The property matters in server code, which is why the domain's HMAC page documents it for that setting.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Hash Comparison Tool", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Compute or locate the first digest.</strong> Hash your content with any generator page (the <a href=\"/developers/sha-256-generator\">SHA-256 Generator</a> for text, the <a href=\"/developers/file-hash-checker\">File Hash Checker</a> for files), or find the published value you are verifying against.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Paste into the two panels.</strong> Computed value in one, published value in the other. Trailing newlines and case differences are handled; there is no need to clean either string first.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Read the verdict.</strong> <code>✓ Match</code> means the normalised strings are identical — for equal-length hex digests, that is byte-level identity of the underlying hashes. <code>✗ Different</code> means at least one byte differs, however invisibly.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — On a mismatch, check lengths first.</strong> Equal-length digests that differ mean the content differs. Different lengths usually mean an algorithm mismatch (40 vs 64 characters — SHA-1 vs SHA-256) or a truncated paste; either way the diagnosis is visible in the strings themselves.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Compare other strings with the same rules in mind.</strong> Version strings, commit IDs, API keys, base64 blobs — any exact-match question where case and edge whitespace are noise. If internal whitespace or case is <em>meaningful</em> to your comparison, this tool's normalisation will mask it — use a diff tool for those.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Download verification.</strong> The canonical flow: hash the downloaded artifact, paste beside the published checksum, read the verdict. Case-insensitivity absorbs the publisher's formatting; the verdict is the integrity answer.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Config and snapshot auditing.</strong> Compare a recorded digest against a fresh computation to confirm a configuration is unchanged — the compact, unambiguous \"has anything touched this?\" check.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Token and key comparisons during development.</strong> Confirming a newly generated API key matches what was pasted into a secrets manager, or that two displayed values are truly identical rather than lookalike.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Cross-tool digest reconciliation.</strong> Different tools print digests in different cases (and occasionally with padding). This tool normalises the cosmetics so the comparison answers the real question.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Spot-the-difference for lookalike strings.</strong> Two strings that render identically but contain different Unicode characters (smart vs straight quotes, homoglyphs) are correctly reported as different — a surprisingly common source of config bugs.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Hash <code>hello world</code> with the SHA-256 generator and you get:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>b94d27b9934d3e08a52e52d7da7dabfac484efe37a5380ee9088f7ace2efcde9</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Paste that into the first panel. Into the second, paste the same digest as a published record might render it — uppercase, with a trailing newline from a PDF copy:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>B94D27B9934D3E08A52E52D7DA7DABFAC484EFE37A5380EE9088F7ACE2EFCDE9\\n</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Run the transform: <code>✓ Match</code>. The case difference and the invisible newline are exactly the noise this tool exists to absorb, and the verdict reflects the underlying hashes being byte-identical.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now the two instructive mismatches. Change one character of the digest — <code>b94d27…efcde8</code> instead of <code>…cde9</code> — and the verdict is <code>✗ Different</code>: for equal-length hex digests, any single-character difference means different content, full stop. And compare digests of different algorithms — 40 characters against 64 — for a different kind of <code>✗</code>: not a content mismatch but an algorithm one, diagnosable from the lengths alone.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Match algorithms before matching strings.</strong> A 40-character SHA-1 digest will never equal a 64-character SHA-256 one. When lengths differ, the fix is upstream — hash with the right algorithm — not closer inspection of the strings.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Internal whitespace is significant.</strong> The tool trims edges only. Two digests pasted with a space in the middle are genuinely different strings and will be reported as such — correctly. Clean the paste instead.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Case-insensitivity is for hex digests.</strong> Base64 digests are case-<em>sensitive</em> (upper and lower case encode different values), and so are most tokens. Comparing base64 with this tool can produce a false match on case alone — know your encoding.</p>",
        "<p><strong>This is not the timing-safe comparison your server needs.</strong> Verifying secrets server-side requires constant-time comparison; this tool is a local browser utility for values you already hold. The distinction matters in code, not here.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Lookalike Unicode is different Unicode.</strong> A smart quote, a Cyrillic а, a non-breaking space — all compare as different characters. When a \"visually identical\" pair reports different, trust the tool and inspect the bytes.</p>",
        "<p><strong>One comparison per run.</strong> The two panels hold one pair. For bulk comparisons — many files against many published digests — command-line tooling iterating the list is the right shape; this page is the single-verdict instrument.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the tool compare hashes or raw content?</h3>It compares whatever two strings you paste — it has no hashing step. The intended use is comparing digest strings, but any exact-match-with-normalisation question works the same way. If you have raw content and a published digest, hash the content first with the matching algorithm, then compare here." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why did identical-looking strings report Different?</h3>The strings differed at the byte level — invisible differences are the tool's speciality. The usual suspects: a smart quote versus a straight quote, a non-breaking space versus a space, a homoglyph from another alphabet, or a zero-width character riding along from a rich-text copy. Trust the verdict and diff the bytes; that is the check working as designed." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I compare two passwords here?</h3>You can, and the comparison is exact — but the right instrument for password matching is your application's bcrypt-compare against the stored hash, not string equality. Comparing password strings directly is itself the anti-pattern that bcrypt's design exists to replace; nothing in a proper credential flow ever compares two plaintext passwords." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is the comparison timing-safe?</h3>The tool performs a local string comparison in your browser — timing side channels are not a meaningful threat model here, because both inputs are already yours. The timing-safe comparison property matters in production verification code, where response timing can leak information to a network observer; server-side checkers should use constant-time comparison, a property documented on the HMAC page for that context." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does it handle Base64 and other encodings?</h3>As strings, yes — with the caveat that case-insensitive comparison is only correct for hex digests. Base64 encodes values case-sensitively, so two Base64 strings differing only in case are genuinely different values, and this tool would wrongly report a match. For Base64 comparisons, use the <a href=\"/developers/base64-encoder-decoder\">Base64 Encoder/Decoder</a> to decode both and compare the decoded bytes." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><a href=\"/developers/sha-256-generator\">SHA-256 Generator</a> and <a href=\"/developers/hash-generator\">Hash Generator</a> produce the digests this tool compares; <a href=\"/developers/file-hash-checker\">File Hash Checker</a> computes all three algorithms for uploads in one pass; <a href=\"/developers/hmac-generator\">HMAC Generator</a> for the keyed constructions whose verification inspired the timing-safety notes; and <a href=\"/developers/base64-encoder-decoder\">Base64 Encoder/Decoder</a> for the case-sensitive encodings this tool deliberately does not judge.</p>" },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("hash-comparison-tool", hash_comparison_toolSeo);

