const jwt_hs256_verifierSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the JWT HS256 Verifier Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The JWT HS256 Verifier performs a real cryptographic signature check on an HMAC-SHA256-signed token — in your browser, with the shared secret you supply. Paste the complete token into the input panel, enter the secret in the <code>Secret</code> field in the options bar, and run the transform. The output is a one-line verdict: <code>\u2713 Signature valid (HS256)</code> when the recomputed signature matches the token's, or <code>\u2717 Signature does NOT match</code> when it does not.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Unlike the RS256 path — where browser-side verification is withheld because key authority matters — HS256 verification is exactly what a browser page can do honestly: the secret is a shared string, the check is a symmetric HMAC comparison, and Web Crypto performs it natively. The tool imports your secret as an HMAC-SHA256 key, computes the signature over the token's <code>header.payload</code> bytes, decodes the token's Base64Url signature, and compares the two. There is no approximation and no placeholder — this is the same operation your auth server performs.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Empty secrets are rejected with an explicit error — the tool will not \"verify\" against an empty key. And the verdict covers only the signature: the tool does not evaluate claims, expiry, issuer, or audience. A perfectly signed token with an expired <code>exp</code> verifies successfully here, which is correct behaviour and the clearest demonstration that authenticity and validity are separate checks.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the HS256 Verification Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>HS256 signatures are HMACs — Hash-based Message Authentication Codes — computed with SHA-256. An HMAC is a keyed hash: it hashes the message twice, once with the key folded into an inner pad and once into an outer pad, producing 32 bytes that are indistinguishable from random to anyone lacking the key. JWTs sign the string <code>header.payload</code> — the Base64Url segments joined by a dot, without a trailing dot.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Verification recomputes that HMAC and compares. The tool's path: import the secret as a raw <code>HMAC / SHA-256</code> key in Web Crypto; Base64Url-decode the token's third segment (mapping <code>-</code> back to <code>+</code>, <code>_</code> to <code>/</code>, restoring stripped padding); then call <code>crypto.subtle.verify</code>, which performs the comparison in constant time — the timing-safe comparison that prevents an attacker from learning how many leading signature bytes matched. The boolean it returns is the verdict.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Constant-time comparison deserves its sentence: a naive byte-by-byte equality check leaks information through how quickly it fails. Web Crypto's verify compares all 32 bytes regardless, so response timing reveals nothing about where a mismatch begins — invisible when present, catastrophic when absent.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>What verification proves, precisely: the holder of the secret produced (or countersigned) this exact header.payload pair. What it does not prove: that the secret holder is who you think it is (did you paste the right secret for this issuer?), that the claims are currently true (the token may be expired or revoked), or that the algorithm is even HS256 — the tool's verification assumes the token's signature segment is an HMAC; a token claiming <code>alg:RS256</code> will simply fail, but the informative failure is the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-rs256-verifier\">JWT RS256 Verifier</a>'s inspection report.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the JWT HS256 Verifier", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Paste the complete token.</strong> All three dot-separated segments, exactly as received. Truncated tokens fail the decode rather than the signature check, so a malformed paste is distinguishable from a wrong secret.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Enter the secret.</strong> In the options bar's <code>Secret</code> field, type the shared secret exactly — case, length, and every character. HMACs are unforgiving: <code>Secret1</code> and <code>secret1</code> produce entirely different signatures.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Run the transform.</strong> One verdict line comes back. No report, no claims dump — the signature either matches or it does not.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Interpret the two outcomes.</strong> A valid signature means this token's header and payload are byte-for-byte what the secret holder signed. A mismatch means one of: the token was modified after signing, the secret is wrong, or the token is not HS256 at all. Work through them in that order.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Check the rest of the token separately.</strong> Signature authenticity does not cover lifetime. Run the same token through the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-validator\">JWT Validator</a> or <a href=\"/developers/jwt-expiry-checker\">JWT Expiry Checker</a> before treating its claims as currently trustworthy.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Confirming a token's provenance during debugging.</strong> When an API accepts your token but behaves oddly, verification separates \"the token is not what the server issued\" from \"the token is genuine but its claims mislead you.\" One run answers the provenance half.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Testing signature-verification logic offline.</strong> Building an auth middleware? Generate tokens with the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-generator\">JWT Generator</a> using a known secret, then verify them here — the round trip exercises the exact trust boundary your middleware implements, without standing up an auth server.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Demonstrating tamper-evidence.</strong> Flip one character in a token's payload and verify: the signature fails, though the token still parses. It is the single most instructive JWT demonstration, and it takes thirty seconds with this tool and the generator.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Sanity-checking secret rotation.</strong> After rotating a signing secret, verify an old token (should fail against the new secret) and a new one (should pass). Both outcomes in one minute beats a day of intermittent auth failures in staging.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Take this token — generated with the secret <code>secret</code>, claims fully readable in its payload:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxMjM0NTY3ODkwIiwibmFtZSI6IkphbmUgRG9lIiwiaWF0IjoxNTE2MjM5MDIyfQ.1VAmegkQOBsQ-iSZU96z6c8NY7QR9OhhYWYHwHTeGT4</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Enter <code>secret</code> in the Secret field and run the transform:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>\u2713 Signature valid (HS256)</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The verification recomputed HMAC-SHA256 over the 36-character header, a dot, and the 74-character payload — using your secret — and compared the 32-byte result against the 43-character Base64Url signature. Identical, hence valid. This token's claims are now cryptographically attributable to whoever held <code>secret</code> when it was signed.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Three failures are worth reproducing, each teaching something different. Change the secret to <code>Secret</code> (capital S): mismatch — HMAC output is wholly different, case matters absolutely. Change one character in the payload segment — say the <code>name</code> claim: mismatch, even though the token still decodes to valid JSON; this is tamper-evidence in action. Keep the secret but paste a token whose header says <code>alg:RS256</code>: mismatch again, and the right response is inspection with the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-rs256-verifier\">JWT RS256 Verifier</a>, not more secret guessing.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Secret mismatch is the most common failure.</strong> Before concluding a token is forged, verify you pasted the secret exactly: no trailing space, no case slip, no smart-quote substitution from copying through a document. Nine wrong-secret reports out of ten are paste artefacts.</p>",
        "<p><strong>A valid signature says nothing about expiry.</strong> The tool checks authenticity only. Expired, revoked, and not-yet-valid tokens all verify successfully — lifetime belongs to the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-validator\">JWT Validator</a>.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Non-HS256 tokens simply fail.</strong> There is no algorithm auto-detection: the verification assumes an HMAC signature. If you suspect the token is RS256 or ES256, inspect its header first rather than concluding the secret is wrong.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Handle the secret with the same care as the token.</strong> The tool runs locally and transmits nothing, but pasting production signing secrets into any browser page deserves the same discipline as pasting the tokens themselves: prefer test secrets, and rotate anything you have pasted somewhere questionable.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Paste the whole token.</strong> The signature covers <code>header.payload</code> exactly; verifying a fragment is meaningless. Whitespace around the token is trimmed, but any edit inside it changes what is signed.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Timing attacks are already handled.</strong> The comparison runs through Web Crypto's constant-time verify, so repeated attempts cannot extract signature bytes by timing. The security boundary here is the secret's confidentiality, nothing else.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is verifying a JWT in the browser actually safe?</h3>The computation is — it happens locally in Web Crypto, with no network involvement, and neither the token nor the secret leaves the page. The operational caution is about the secret, not the maths: pasting a production signing secret into any browser context is a decision to make deliberately. For production secrets, prefer verifying on infrastructure you control; use this tool with test secrets, or with secrets you are prepared to rotate." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>The signature fails but I am sure the secret is right — what else?</h3>In rough order of likelihood: the token was modified after signing (even one payload character); the token is not HS256 (check the header's <code>alg</code>); the token was truncated or whitespace-mangled in transit; or the secret genuinely differs — different environment, rotation timing, or a staging/production split. The <a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a> shows the header's algorithm when you need to rule the second cause in or out." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does an expired token still verify as valid?</h3>Because signature validity and temporal validity are independent properties, and conflating them is how auth bugs hide. The signature proves the issuer signed these claims at some point; it says nothing about whether those claims are current. Production systems verify the signature first and then check <code>exp</code>, <code>nbf</code>, and revocation separately — this tool deliberately mirrors the first step only." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I verify RS256 or ES256 tokens here?</h3>No — the tool performs HMAC verification only. RS256 verification needs the issuer's RSA public key and the RSA maths, which belongs in your service where key trust is enforceable; the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-rs256-verifier\">JWT RS256 Verifier</a> provides the inspection half of that workflow. ES256 (ECDSA) likewise requires its own key type and verification path." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the tool tell me which claims failed?</h3>No claim is evaluated at all — the output is purely the signature verdict. If you need the claims beside the verdict, decode the token first with the <a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a>, then verify here. Keeping the two outputs separate mirrors how verification and validation actually compose in production code." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><a href=\"/developers/jwt-generator\">JWT Generator</a> mints HS256 tokens with a secret you choose — the natural counterpart for round-trip testing; <a href=\"/developers/jwt-decoder\">JWT Decoder</a> reveals the claims behind a verdict; <a href=\"/developers/jwt-validator\">JWT Validator</a> and <a href=\"/developers/jwt-expiry-checker\">JWT Expiry Checker</a> cover the lifetime checks verification leaves out; and <a href=\"/developers/hmac-generator\">HMAC Generator</a> exposes the raw HMAC primitive underneath, if you want to see the signature computation without the JWT framing.</p>" },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("jwt-hs256-verifier", jwt_hs256_verifierSeo);

const hash_generatorSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Hash Generator Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Hash Generator computes a cryptographic hash of any text using the browser's Web Crypto API. Paste your input, pick an algorithm from the selector — <code>SHA-1</code>, <code>SHA-256</code>, <code>SHA-384</code>, or <code>SHA-512</code> — and the output panel shows the digest as a lowercase hexadecimal string. An info line reports the algorithm used and the input's size in bytes, so you always know what was hashed.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The four choices are exactly what Web Crypto's <code>subtle.digest</code> supports for general hashing — this tool deliberately does not offer MD5, which Web Crypto omits because MD5 is cryptographically broken. If you specifically need an MD5 digest for a legacy non-security purpose, the <a href=\"/developers/md5-generator\">MD5 Generator</a> implements it directly. Likewise the SHA-1 page exists separately because SHA-1, while still in the API, carries its own collision history worth understanding before you rely on it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Hashing is one-way by construction. The digest is a fixed-length fingerprint — 160 bits for SHA-1, 256 for SHA-256, 384 for SHA-384, 512 for SHA-512, regardless of whether the input is one character or one million. You cannot recover the input from the hash; you can only hash a candidate input and compare. That asymmetry is the entire point, and it is what makes the digest useful for integrity checks, fingerprints, and deduplication — and useless for storing anything you will need to read back.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Hash Generator Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>All four algorithms are members of the Merkle–Damgård family: the input is padded to a multiple of the block size, split into blocks, and each block is mixed into a running internal state seeded by a fixed constant. The final state is the digest. The variants differ in two knobs — the digest width and the number of rounds — and those knobs produce the practical differences you see in the output lengths.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>SHA-256, the workhorse, processes 512-bit blocks through 64 rounds of arithmetic over eight 32-bit words and emits 256 bits (64 hex characters). SHA-512 scales everything up — 1024-bit blocks, 80 rounds, eight 64-bit words, 128 hex characters — which is also why SHA-512 can be <em>faster</em> than SHA-256 on 64-bit processors: fewer rounds over wider words. SHA-384 is SHA-512 with the output truncated to 384 bits, a convenient width for HMAC use. SHA-1 is the 1995-era predecessor: 160-bit digest, and structurally weakened in ways the SHA-2 family was designed to avoid.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Mechanically the tool is a thin, honest wrapper. Your text is encoded to UTF-8 bytes — worth remembering, because the hash is of the <em>bytes</em>, and a multi-byte character like <code>é</code> contributes two bytes — then passed to <code>crypto.subtle.digest</code> with the selected algorithm, and the resulting 32/48/64-byte buffer is rendered as two-digit hex per byte. Nothing is added to the input: no salt, no key, no secret. The same input always yields the same digest on every machine, which is exactly what makes hashes comparable across systems.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That determinism cuts both ways: it lets you verify a download or deduplicate a file, and it means a hash leaks structure — identical inputs produce identical outputs, and unsalted hashes of passwords fall to precomputed tables. Hashing identifies; it does not conceal. Where a secret must be involved, that is HMAC's job.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Hash Generator", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Paste or type the input.</strong> Any text. Whitespace is part of the input: a trailing newline changes the hash completely, which is the most common reason two hashes of \"the same\" text disagree.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Choose the algorithm.</strong> The selector offers SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384, SHA-512, defaulting to SHA-256. For new work SHA-256 is the standard choice; pick SHA-512 for the wider digest, SHA-384 when a spec calls for it, and SHA-1 only when a legacy format demands it.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Read the digest.</strong> The output is lowercase hex — 40 characters for SHA-1, 64 for SHA-256, 96 for SHA-384, 128 for SHA-512. The info line repeats the algorithm and the byte count of what you hashed.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Compare digests, not inputs.</strong> When verifying data against a published hash, compare the strings directly or paste both into the <a href=\"/developers/hash-comparison-tool\">Hash Comparison Tool</a>. Case differences in hex are the usual cosmetic mismatch — the comparison tool ignores them.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Re-run to confirm determinism.</strong> The digest is identical every run — no salt, no randomness. Outputs that must differ (passwords, tokens) belong to keyed or salted constructions, not this tool.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Verifying downloads and published artifacts.</strong> Software distributions publish SHA-256 digests alongside releases. Hash the file's text content here and compare against the published value to confirm nothing was corrupted or substituted in transit. (For binary files see the caveat on the <a href=\"/developers/file-hash-checker\">File Hash Checker</a>, which computes all three SHA variants in one pass.)</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Cache keys and deduplication.</strong> Hash a payload and use the digest as the storage key: identical content collapses to identical keys, and changed content cannot collide.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Fingerprinting configuration and API payloads.</strong> Before a migration or a refactor, hash the current config or a canonical request body; after, hash again and compare. The two digests differing is an early-warning signal that something semantic changed even when the diff looks innocent.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Understanding digest lengths when integrating.</strong> If a spec says \"SHA-384, hex-encoded,\" this tool shows you what that string looks like — 96 characters — before you write code against it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Teaching what one-way means.</strong> Hash a sentence, then try to derive the sentence from the digest. The exercise makes the asymmetry concrete in a way descriptions rarely do.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Hash the canonical test string <code>hello world</code> with SHA-256 (the default):</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>b94d27b9934d3e08a52e52d7da7dabfac484efe37a5380ee9088f7ace2efcde9</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That is the exact digest every conforming SHA-256 implementation produces for those eleven bytes — Node's <code>crypto</code>, OpenSSL, Python's <code>hashlib</code>, this tool's Web Crypto. The info line reads <code>SHA-256 · 11 bytes input</code>. Switch the selector to SHA-1 and the same input yields <code>2aae6c35c94fcfb415dbe95f408b9ce91ee846ed</code> — 40 characters, a different fingerprint from a different construction. Both are correct; they are simply different functions.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now the sensitivity demonstration, which is the fastest way to internalise the avalanche property. Add a single trailing space and re-run: the SHA-256 digest changes in roughly half its bits, to <code>3d583c65db6e2a0d8c7af0d0bfeab0d0c5f6b4012e5e4b2e1f9e5a0b3b7d4a1f</code>-shaped output bearing no visible relationship to the first. No partial similarity survives; the two digests are unrelated strings. Compare two hashes of \"the same\" document that differ at all, and the honest conclusion is the documents differ — even a single invisible byte of whitespace.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Whitespace is input.</strong> Trailing newlines from copy-paste, a final period added by an editor, CRLF versus LF line endings — each changes the hash. When two digests of \"identical\" text disagree, diff the bytes before doubting the algorithm.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Pick SHA-2 family for new work; treat SHA-1 as legacy.</strong> SHA-1 remains available because formats still specify it, but collision attacks against it are practical. Nothing new should depend on its collision resistance.</p>",
        "<p><strong>A plain hash is not password storage.</strong> Unsalted, unkeyed digests of passwords fall to rainbow tables at scale. Salted, deliberately slow hashing — bcrypt — is the tool for credentials, and this domain provides it.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Hex encoding doubles the length.</strong> The digest itself is 32 bytes for SHA-256; the 64-character string is its hex representation. If an API expects raw bytes and you paste hex (or vice versa), mismatches look like wrong hashes but are wrong encodings.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Do not roll comparisons yourself in production code.</strong> This tool compares by eye and by the comparison tool for humans; server-side verification should use a constant-time comparison so response timing cannot leak how many bytes matched.</p>",
        "<p><strong>The digest proves integrity, not authenticity.</strong> Anyone can recompute the hash of a modified file. Proving <em>who</em> produced the data requires a keyed construction — HMAC — or a signature.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why is there no MD5 option?</h3>MD5 is cryptographically broken and Web Crypto deliberately omits it. For legacy non-security digests, the <a href=\"/developers/md5-generator\">MD5 Generator</a> implements the algorithm directly." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Which algorithm should I choose?</h3>For new integrations, SHA-256: it is the default assumption of most specs, fast enough for any interactive use, and 64 hex characters. Choose SHA-384 or SHA-512 when a specification names them or when you want the wider margin; SHA-512 can even be faster on 64-bit hardware for large inputs. SHA-1 only for reading legacy formats — never for new signatures or integrity guarantees." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is the output the same on every computer?</h3>Yes — that is the property. SHA-256 of the same bytes is the same 64 hex characters in every conforming implementation: this browser tool, Node, OpenSSL, Python, Java. If two implementations disagree on the same input, the input bytes are not actually the same — encoding and whitespace are the usual culprits." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I hash a file here?</h3>This page hashes pasted text; the <a href=\"/developers/file-hash-checker\">File Hash Checker</a> handles uploads with all three SHA algorithms in one pass — with its documented text-mode caveat for binary files." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does hashing reveal what the input was?</h3>Not directly — you cannot invert a digest — but plain hashing does not hide low-entropy inputs. A hash of a password, a small ID, or a name from a short list can be confirmed by hashing candidates until one matches. That is why salted, keyed, or deliberately slow constructions exist for anything an attacker could guess. For arbitrary long text, the digest leaks effectively nothing." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><a href=\"/developers/sha-256-generator\">SHA-256 Generator</a>, <a href=\"/developers/sha-512-generator\">SHA-512 Generator</a>, and <a href=\"/developers/sha-1-generator\">SHA-1 Generator</a> are the single-algorithm pages with deeper per-algorithm notes; <a href=\"/developers/md5-generator\">MD5 Generator</a> covers the legacy digest; <a href=\"/developers/hmac-generator\">HMAC Generator</a> adds the secret key that turns a fingerprint into an authenticator; <a href=\"/developers/bcrypt-generator\">Bcrypt Generator</a> is the credential-storage tool; <a href=\"/developers/file-hash-checker\">File Hash Checker</a> hashes uploads in one pass; and <a href=\"/developers/hash-comparison-tool\">Hash Comparison Tool</a> compares two digests case-insensitively.</p>" },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("hash-generator", hash_generatorSeo);

const caesar_cipher_toolSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What is Caesar Cipher Tool", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The Caesar Cipher Tool implements the classical Caesar substitution cipher, where each letter in the input is shifted forward or backward by a configurable number of positions in the alphabet. Named after Julius Caesar, who used a shift of 3 for military correspondence, this is one of the oldest known encryption techniques. The tool supports shifts from 1 to 25, wraps around the alphabet (so shifting Z by 1 produces A), and preserves case and non-alphabetic characters exactly as they appear." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "While the Caesar cipher offers zero practical security by modern standards — it is trivially broken by brute force (only 25 possible shifts) or frequency analysis — it remains a valuable educational tool for understanding how substitution ciphers work and for solving cryptography puzzles, escape room challenges, and CTF (Capture The Flag) competitions." },
    ]},
    { heading: "How to Use Caesar Cipher Tool", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<strong>Enter your plaintext</strong> in the input panel. This can be any English text, a puzzle clue, or a message you want to encode.",
        "<strong>Set the shift value</strong> using the number input or slider. A positive shift encrypts (shifts forward); the tool also supports decoding by applying the reverse shift.",
        "<strong>Read the output</strong> in the output panel. The cipher text appears instantly as you adjust the shift. Non-letter characters (spaces, punctuation, numbers) pass through unchanged.",
        "<strong>Try all 25 shifts</strong> if you are solving a puzzle and do not know the key — the tool makes it easy to cycle through possibilities until the output reads as meaningful English.",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Why Use Caesar Cipher Tool", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The most practical use case today is <strong>solving cryptography puzzles</strong>. Escape rooms, puzzle hunts, and introductory cryptography courses frequently use Caesar ciphers as warm-up challenges. Instead of manually shifting each letter, you can paste the cipher text and try shifts programmatically, which is faster and eliminates counting errors." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Educators use this tool to <strong>demonstrate the weakness of simple substitution</strong>. By showing students that any Caesar-encoded message can be broken in seconds, it motivates the need for stronger algorithms like AES. Game developers also use it for <strong>in-game secret messages</strong> and easter eggs where the cipher is meant to be solvable by players." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Privacy & Security", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This tool runs entirely in your browser — no data ever leaves your device. There is no server round-trip, no upload, no logging, and no account required. Your input is processed locally using client-side JavaScript and is never stored, transmitted, or accessible to anyone else. When you close the tab, everything disappears." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", subHeading: "Is the Caesar cipher secure?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "No. The Caesar cipher is one of the weakest encryption methods in existence. With only 25 possible shifts, an attacker can try every one in seconds. Even without brute force, <strong>frequency analysis</strong> — comparing the letter distribution of the cipher text against known English letter frequencies — reveals the shift in a single step. Never use the Caesar cipher for anything that requires actual security." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "What is the difference between Caesar cipher and ROT13?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "ROT13 is simply a Caesar cipher with a fixed shift of 13. It has the special property that applying it twice returns the original text (since 13 + 13 = 26, a full alphabet rotation). This tool lets you choose any shift from 1 to 25, making it more flexible than a dedicated ROT13 tool, though you can achieve ROT13 by setting the shift to 13." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Can this tool decode messages encoded with a different shift?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Yes — if you know the shift value used to encode the message, set the tool to that shift and apply the reverse operation. If you do not know the shift, try all 25 values until the output makes sense. For longer messages, the correct shift will produce output that matches English letter frequency patterns, while incorrect shifts will produce gibberish." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Does it handle numbers and punctuation?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Yes. Only alphabetic characters (A-Z, a-z) are shifted. Digits, spaces, punctuation, and special characters are preserved exactly as they appear in the input. This means encoded messages retain their original formatting, which can be useful for puzzle contexts where punctuation is a hint." },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("caesar-cipher-tool", caesar_cipher_toolSeo);

const rsa_key_generatorSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What is RSA Key Generator", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The RSA Key Generator creates a matched pair of RSA public and private keys directly in your browser using the Web Crypto API's <code>SubtleCrypto.generateKey</code> interface. It supports key sizes of 1024, 2048, and 4096 bits, with 2048-bit being the current recommended minimum for production use. The output includes both keys in PEM format — the standard text encoding used by OpenSSL, SSH, and most certificate management tools." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "RSA (Rivest–Shamir–Adleman) is an asymmetric cryptographic algorithm: the public key encrypts data, and only the corresponding private key can decrypt it. This tool generates fresh key pairs for each request, meaning the private key exists only in your browser during the generation session and is never transmitted to any server." },
    ]},
    { heading: "How to Use RSA Key Generator", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<strong>Select the key size</strong> from the options panel. 2048-bit is the default and recommended for most applications. Use 4096-bit for high-security requirements (at the cost of slower generation and encryption operations).",
        "<strong>Click Generate</strong> to create the key pair. Generation typically takes 1-5 seconds for 2048-bit keys and up to 15 seconds for 4096-bit keys, depending on your device.",
        "<strong>Copy the public key</strong> — this is the key you share with others or embed in configuration files. It begins with <code>-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----</code>.",
        "<strong>Copy the private key</strong> — this must remain confidential. It begins with <code>-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----</code>. Store it in a secure location, never in source control or shared drives.",
        "<strong>Use the keys</strong> in your application, SSH configuration, or certificate signing workflow. The PEM format is compatible with OpenSSL, most programming languages, and CI/CD pipelines.",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Why Use RSA Key Generator", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Developers need RSA key pairs for several concrete workflows. <strong>SSH key authentication</strong> is the most common — generating a fresh key pair for a new server or CI/CD pipeline, then copying the public key to the authorized_keys file. <strong>JWT RS256 signing</strong> requires an RSA key pair where the private key signs tokens and the public key verifies them. <strong>API authentication</strong> systems like GitHub's JWT-based apps and certain OAuth flows also require RSA keys." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Using a browser-based generator means you can create keys on any machine — including managed workstations where installing OpenSSL or ssh-keygen may require admin privileges. Since generation happens locally, there is no risk of a server-side key escrow or logging incident." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Privacy & Security", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This tool runs entirely in your browser — no data ever leaves your device. There is no server round-trip, no upload, no logging, and no account required. Your input is processed locally using client-side JavaScript and is never stored, transmitted, or accessible to anyone else. When you close the tab, everything disappears." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", subHeading: "Which key size should I choose?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<strong>2048-bit</strong> is the current industry standard and is recommended for most use cases. It provides adequate security against known attacks and is supported by all major libraries and platforms. <strong>4096-bit</strong> offers a higher security margin but generates slower and produces larger keys — use it when compliance requirements mandate it or for long-term secrets (like root CA keys) that need to remain secure for decades." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Are these keys secure enough for production?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The keys themselves are cryptographically sound — generated using the browser's native CSPRNG (Cryptographically Secure Pseudo-Random Number Generator). However, for production use, consider whether you need a <strong>key management strategy</strong>: who has access to the private key, how is it rotated, and how is it stored? A key generated in a browser tab is fine for development and testing, but production private keys should ideally live in a hardware security module (HSM) or a managed secrets vault." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Can I use these keys with OpenSSL?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Yes. The output PEM format is directly compatible with OpenSSL commands like <code>openssl rsa -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem</code>. You can also use the keys with <code>ssh-keygen</code> (after converting the format), with Java's <code>keytool</code>, or with any language that supports PKCS#8 private keys and SPKI public keys." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Why not just use ssh-keygen or openssl?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Those tools are excellent and should be your first choice when available. This browser-based generator exists for situations where command-line access is restricted — managed corporate laptops, air-gapped environments, or quick one-off key generation where opening a terminal feels like overkill. It produces equivalent keys in the same standard format." },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("rsa-key-generator", rsa_key_generatorSeo);

const password_strength_checkerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What is Password Strength Checker", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The Password Strength Checker evaluates how resistant a password is to brute-force and dictionary attacks by analyzing its entropy, length, character diversity, and pattern weaknesses. It provides a real-time strength score (Weak, Fair, Good, Strong, Very Strong) along with a detailed breakdown of what contributes to or detracts from the password's security. The analysis runs locally — your password is never transmitted, logged, or compared against any external service." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Unlike simple length checkers, this tool applies Shannon entropy estimation and detects common patterns like keyboard walks (qwerty), repeated characters (aaa), dictionary words, and date formats. This gives you a more accurate picture of how long the password would withstand a modern cracking rig running billions of attempts per second." },
    ]},
    { heading: "How to Use Password Strength Checker", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<strong>Type or paste a password</strong> into the input field. The strength meter updates in real time as you type — no button click required.",
        "<strong>Read the strength rating</strong> displayed next to the input. The rating changes dynamically based on the password's characteristics.",
        "<strong>Review the detailed breakdown</strong> below the meter, which shows entropy bits, estimated crack time, and specific pattern warnings (e.g., 'contains dictionary word' or 'keyboard walk detected').",
        "<strong>Use the suggestions</strong> to improve weak passwords — the tool tells you exactly which weakness to address, whether it is length, character variety, or a detected pattern.",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Why Use Password Strength Checker", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The primary use case is <strong>evaluating passwords before you adopt them</strong>. When generating a new password — whether manually or from a password manager — running it through this checker tells you whether it actually meets modern security thresholds. Many people underestimate how fast specialized hardware can crack passwords: an 8-character password with lowercase letters only can be brute-forced in minutes on modern GPUs, while a 16-character mixed-case password with symbols would take centuries." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Security teams use this tool during <strong>password policy reviews</strong> to test whether their organization's minimum requirements (e.g., '8 characters with one uppercase') actually produce strong passwords. Developers use it to <strong>validate generated API keys and tokens</strong> before committing them to configuration files. It is also useful for <strong>educating users</strong> — showing someone in real time how adding just four more characters moves their password from 'cracked in hours' to 'cracked in centuries' is a powerful demonstration." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Privacy & Security", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This tool runs entirely in your browser — no data ever leaves your device. There is no server round-trip, no upload, no logging, and no account required. Your input is processed locally using client-side JavaScript and is never stored, transmitted, or accessible to anyone else. When you close the tab, everything disappears." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", subHeading: "How is the strength score calculated?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The score combines several factors: <strong>password length</strong> (the single most important factor), <strong>character set diversity</strong> (uppercase, lowercase, digits, symbols), <strong>Shannon entropy</strong> (a measure of information content per character), and <strong>pattern detection</strong> (dictionary words, keyboard walks, repeated characters, date formats). The final rating maps the composite entropy score to a five-level scale aligned with NIST SP 800-63B guidelines." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Is my password sent to any server?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "No. All analysis happens locally in your browser using JavaScript. The password never leaves your device, is never stored in any form, and is never compared against any external database or breach list. You can verify this by disconnecting from the internet — the tool works identically offline." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "What makes a password 'strong'?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Modern password security favors <strong>length over complexity</strong>. A 20-character passphrase using only lowercase words (like 'correct horse battery staple') is significantly stronger than an 8-character password with mixed case, numbers, and symbols. The ideal password is long (16+ characters), avoids dictionary words and personal information, and uses a random mix of character types. Password managers generate passwords that meet all three criteria effortlessly." },
    ]},
    { heading: "", subHeading: "Should I use this to check my existing passwords?", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Yes — but be aware that typing your actual password into any tool carries some risk if your machine is compromised (keyloggers, screen recording). For existing passwords, consider using your password manager's built-in strength audit instead, which accesses your vault directly without requiring you to type the password. This tool is best suited for evaluating <strong>new</strong> passwords before you adopt them." },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("password-strength-checker", password_strength_checkerSeo);

