const websocket_testerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What is Websocket Tester", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The WebSocket Tester is a client-side tool that establishes WebSocket connections to specified servers and provides an interactive interface for sending and receiving messages. It features a URL input field (defaulting to wss://echo.websocket.org), a Connect button, a Send button (which sends the text \"ping\"), a Close button, and a scrollable log panel that displays all connection events: successful connection, received messages, disconnection with close code, and errors. The tool creates a new WebSocket instance with the specified URL, attaches event handlers for open, message, close, and error events, and displays each event in the log with a status indicator (checkmark for success, X for errors/closure). The Send button sends a predefined \"ping\" message — this is a simple test to verify the server responds. The Close button gracefully closes the connection with the default close code. This tool is essential for debugging WebSocket-based applications, testing server connectivity, and verifying that WebSocket endpoints are reachable and responding.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How to Use Websocket Tester", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Enter a WebSocket URL in the input field (e.g., wss://echo.websocket.org or ws://localhost:8080). The URL must start with ws:// or wss://.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Click Connect. The tool creates a WebSocket connection and logs the result. A checkmark entry indicates successful connection; an X indicates failure.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Once connected, click Send to transmit a test message (\"ping\"). If the server echoes responses, they will appear in the log prefixed with a left arrow.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4:</strong> To end the session, click Close. The tool logs the close code and the connection is terminated. You can reconnect by clicking Connect again.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Why Use Websocket Tester", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>WebSocket connections are notoriously difficult to debug because they operate outside the normal HTTP request/response cycle. When a WebSocket fails to connect, the issue could be the URL, authentication, network policy, server configuration, or protocol mismatch. The WebSocket Tester isolates the connection itself — by testing with a simple echo server first, you can confirm that WebSocket connectivity works in general before debugging your specific application's WebSocket logic. The log panel provides immediate visibility into the connection lifecycle, which is especially valuable for diagnosing connection drops, unexpected close codes, and message delivery failures.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Privacy & Security", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This tool runs entirely in your browser — no data ever leaves your device. There is no server round-trip, no upload, no logging, and no account required. Your input is processed locally using client-side JavaScript and is never stored, transmitted, or accessible to anyone else. When you close the tab, everything disappears." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does the echo.websocket.org server not respond?</h3><p>The echo.websocket.org service may be unavailable or deprecated. Try using a local WebSocket server or another echo service for testing. The tool works with any WebSocket endpoint.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I send custom messages?</h3><p>The current Send button sends a hardcoded \"ping\" message. For custom messages, you would need to modify the tool to accept user input for the message body.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does it support binary messages?</h3><p>The tool sends and receives text messages only. Binary WebSocket frames (ArrayBuffer, Blob) are not currently handled in the log display.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does the connection fail immediately?</h3><p>Common causes: the server is not running, the URL is wrong (check ws:// vs wss://), a firewall is blocking the connection, or the server requires specific subprotocol headers that the tool does not send.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I test authentication-protected WebSockets?</h3><p>Not directly — the tool does not support custom headers or authentication tokens in the WebSocket handshake. For authenticated connections, you would need to extend the tool or use a library like Socket.IO.</p>" },
    ]},
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("websocket-tester", websocket_testerSeo);

const regex_testerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Tester Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Tester runs one regular expression against a block of text and prints a match report: every match it found, the character index where each one starts, and the contents of every capture group. It is deliberately a reporting tool rather than a highlighter. You get one line per match in the form <code>[0] \"matched text\" at index 12</code>, with a second line listing capture groups as a JSON array whenever the pattern contains parentheses.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That distinction matters. A highlighter shows you where matches are; a match report also tells you <em>what your groups actually captured</em>, which is the part developers usually get wrong. A pattern like <code>(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})</code> can look correct on screen while the first group silently swallows a different span than you intended. Seeing the group array printed next to the match is the fastest way to catch that.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The tester is useful at two moments: while you are still shaping a pattern and need fast feedback, and after a pattern has already shipped and is misbehaving in production, when you need to run it against the exact input that broke it.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Regex Tester Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Underneath, the tool calls JavaScript's built-in <code>RegExp</code> constructor with your pattern and the flags you toggled, then walks the subject text with <code>matchAll()</code>. Nothing is reimplemented, which is the most important fact about this tool: <strong>you are testing the exact engine that will run your pattern in production</strong> — V8's Irregexp in Chrome and Edge, SpiderMonkey in Firefox, JavaScriptCore in Safari.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Because it targets ECMAScript, the dialect differs from what you may expect from other ecosystems. This is an ECMAScript pattern, not PCRE, Python or .NET. Lookarounds are supported, named capture groups use <code>(?<name>...)</code>, and Unicode property escapes like <code>\\p{Letter}</code> require the <code>u</code> flag. Possessive quantifiers and atomic groups do not exist, and neither do <code>\\A</code> or <code>\\Z</code>. A pattern pasted from a PHP or Python codebase frequently fails here for exactly those reasons — and it will fail in your JavaScript too, which is the point.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>One implementation detail is worth knowing before you trust a sticky-flag result. Matching is done through <code>matchAll()</code>, an API that only exists for global regexes, so the tool appends <code>g</code> to your flag set internally. That is why the default flags read <code>g</code> and why results keep coming after the first match even if you toggle <code>g</code> off.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The reported index is a UTF-16 code-unit offset, the same unit <code>String.prototype.indexOf</code> returns. For text outside the Basic Multilingual Plane — emoji, some CJK extensions, mathematical alphanumerics — one visible character occupies two offsets, so an index of 14 may be the eleventh visible glyph.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>An invalid pattern does not return a report. The constructor throws and the tool surfaces the engine's own message, prefixed with <code>Invalid regex:</code>. Those messages are precise and worth reading closely — <em>Invalid regular expression: /a{2,1}/: numbers out of order in {} quantifier</em> tells you the engine rejected the pattern before matching ever began.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Tester", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Load your subject text.</strong> Paste the text you want to search into the main input area. In this tool the textarea holds the <em>subject</em>, not the pattern. Paste the wrong thing into this box and you will see <code>0 matches</code> no matter how correct your pattern is.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Type the pattern.</strong> In the options bar, use the field labelled <strong>Pattern</strong> (its placeholder is <code>\\d+</code>). Enter the pattern body only. Do not wrap it in slashes and do not append flags here; a pasted <code>/\\d+/g</code> becomes a literal slash match.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Toggle flags.</strong> The <strong>Flags</strong> row offers six buttons — <code>g</code>, <code>i</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>u</code>, <code>y</code>. Active flags render filled. Clicking one adds or removes it, and the set is re-sorted alphabetically after each click, so the toggle order does not matter.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Read the report.</strong> Each match produces a line numbered from <code>[0]</code>, showing the matched text in quotes and its start index. Patterns with capture groups add an indented <code>groups:</code> line holding the array of captured values.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Read the count.</strong> The info line reports the total as <code>N match(es)</code>, or <code>0 matches</code>. Use the copy control to take the whole report into a bug ticket or a code review comment.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Shaping a pattern before it ships.</strong> Write the expression here, run it against representative input, and confirm the group array contains what you expect before embedding it in a parser, a validator or a router.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Extracting values from pasted output.</strong> When a log excerpt, an API response dump or a database export reaches you as a blob of text and you need one field out of every line — request IDs, status codes, timestamps, order numbers — a single pattern with a capture group gives you the full list at once.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Debugging a pattern that regressed.</strong> Pin down the smallest input that fails and run it here. Because the report names the index, you can tell immediately whether the failure is about <em>what</em> matched or merely <em>where</em> it matched.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Porting a pattern between languages.</strong> Paste the foreign pattern, watch it throw or misbehave, and adjust it to ECMAScript syntax. Reading the engine's rejection message is faster than consulting a flavour-comparison table.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Checking greediness on realistic data.</strong> A pattern that behaves on a three-word sample can consume an entire line on a real payload. Testing against the real shape of the data before release catches that class of bug cheaply.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Suppose you are pulling order identifiers out of a queue report. Paste this into the subject textarea:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>order 1042 shipped, order 2077 pending</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Set <strong>Pattern</strong> to <code>order (\\d+)</code> and leave the default <code>g</code> flag active. The report reads:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>[0] \"order 1042\" at index 0<br/>  groups: [\"1042\"]<br/>[1] \"order 2077\" at index 20<br/>  groups: [\"2077\"]</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The count line reports <code>2 match(es)</code>. Both matches include the word <em>order</em> because the whole pattern is the match; only the parenthesised part became group one. If you want the numbers alone, capture the digits and ignore the full match — or change the pattern to <code>\\b\\d{4}\\b</code>, which yields <code>[0] \"1042\" at index 6</code> and <code>[1] \"2077\" at index 26</code> with no groups at all.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>A third variant shows the value of the flags row. Change the pattern to <code>^order\\s+(\\d+)</code> with only <code>g</code> active and you get one match, because <code>^</code> anchors to the start of the subject. Toggle <code>m</code> as well and the behaviour is unchanged here, since the subject is a single line; the moment your paste contains newlines, <code>m</code> is what lets <code>^</code> treat each of them as a separate beginning.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>In source code, double the backslashes.</strong> The pattern field takes a raw pattern, so you type <code>\\d</code> here. In a JavaScript string literal the same pattern must be written <code>\"\\\\d\"</code>, because the string parser consumes one backslash before the regex engine ever sees it. A pattern that works in this tester and fails in your file is almost always this.</li><li><strong>Overlapping matches do not exist.</strong> Matching resumes after the <em>end</em> of the previous match, so the pattern <code>ana</code> finds one match in <code>banana</code>, not two. If you need overlaps you must scan with an explicit advancing index instead.</li><li><strong>Sticky <code>y</code> is stricter than you may expect.</strong> With <code>y</code> active, every match must begin exactly where the previous one ended, so <code>\\d+</code> with <code>gy</code> only matches a run of digits at the very start of the subject.</li><li><strong>Catastrophic backtracking is a real risk.</strong> This is a backtracking engine with no step limit and no timeout. A pattern like <code>(a+)+$</code> tested against a long non-matching string can lock the tab. Prefer explicit alternatives and possessive-style rewrites such as <code>(?:a+)+</code> avoidance, and test with the largest realistic input.</li><li><strong>The <code>s</code> flag changes what a dot means.</strong> Without it, <code>.</code> will not cross a newline. A multiline pattern that quietly returns no matches is very often missing either <code>s</code> or <code>m</code>.</li><li><strong>The <code>u</code> flag makes the engine stricter, not just smarter.</strong> It enables code-point semantics — surrogate pairs behave as one character — and simultaneously turns some previously tolerated escapes into syntax errors.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Which regex flavour does this tester use?</h3>ECMAScript, via the browser's own <code>RegExp</code> engine. That is deliberate: it is the same engine your shipped JavaScript will use. Lookarounds and named groups work in current Chrome, Firefox and Safari. Possessive quantifiers, atomic groups <code>(?>...)</code> and Python's <code>(?P<name>...)</code> group syntax will not, and neither will <code>\\A</code> or <code>\\Z</code>." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does my pattern fail here but work in another tool?</h3>The other tool probably uses a different dialect. Most online regex testers offer a flavour selector and quietly default to something PCRE-like. If a pattern relies on a Perl-only construct it will pass there and throw here — and throw in your production JavaScript as well." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does <code>^</code> not match the second line of my input?</h3>Because the <code>m</code> flag is off. Without it, <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> treat the whole subject as a single string and anchor only at its absolute beginning and end." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I paste a JavaScript literal like <code>/\\d+/gi</code>?</h3>No. Split it first: the pattern body <code>\\d+</code> goes in the Pattern field and the modifiers go in the Flags row. Tester fields take the bare pattern, without delimiters." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the tester show named capture groups by name?</h3>It prints capture groups positionally, as a JSON array, so a named group appears as its value in array order rather than under its name. Use the array position to identify it, or test the pattern with the Regex Explainer if you need the structure spelled out." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why do I see zero matches for a pattern I am certain is correct?</h3>Three causes cover almost every case: the subject text was pasted into the Pattern field by mistake, the flags are missing <code>m</code> or <code>s</code>, or the pattern was copied with its slashes and flags still attached." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Once a pattern matches correctly here, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-replace-tester\">Regex Replace Tester</a> shows what it would transform using the same engine and the same flag toggles. If you need the pattern's components spelled out rather than its results, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> lists the token types it recognises. To build an initial pattern from sample lines, start with the <a href=\"/developers/regex-generator\">Regex Generator</a> and verify the result back here. For ready-made starting points covering email addresses, URLs, IPv4 addresses, phone numbers, hex colours and ISO dates, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a> returns a recipe you can paste straight into the Pattern field.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-tester", regex_testerSeo);

const regex_generatorSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Generator Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Generator turns <strong>a list of sample lines into a first-draft pattern</strong>. You paste several strings that look the way your real data looks — one per line — and it derives a pattern that matches that shape, so you do not start from an empty field.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Be clear about what it is not. It does not read a description in English and synthesise a pattern, and it does not learn generally from your examples. It applies a specific, deliberately simple heuristic that is worth understanding, because knowing the rule lets you predict both when it will help and when it will mislead you.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Used correctly it is a scaffold: it gives you a pattern skeleton, and you sharpen it in the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> against a wider sample. Used as an oracle it will disappoint you, because it only looks at a narrow slice of what your examples contain.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Regex Generator Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The algorithm runs in three moves, and every output it produces can be traced back to them.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>First, it trims each line down.</strong> For every line it discards everything from the first character that is not a letter or a digit onward. A line like <code>user_1042</code> becomes <code>user</code>; a line like <code>inv-2024-00417</code> becomes <code>inv</code>. This is why the derived pattern never contains the separators, digits or punctuation you can plainly see in your samples.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Second, it finds the longest common prefix</strong> across those trimmed values, comparing them character by character and shrinking the candidate until every line starts with it. If your lines trim to <code>user</code>, <code>user</code> and <code>user</code>, the prefix is <code>user</code>. If they trim to <code>user</code> and <code>admin</code>, the common prefix is empty and this part contributes nothing.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Third, it appends a digit wildcard if it has earned one.</strong> If every original line contains at least one digit anywhere, it adds <code>\\d+</code> to the pattern. If the prefix was empty and no digit rule applied, it falls back to the permissive catch-all <code>[\\w\\s]+</code>.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Two consequences follow. The prefix is escaped for regex metacharacters before it is emitted, so a sample like <code>a.b</code> correctly produces <code>a\\.b</code> rather than a dot-any pattern. And the output carries its own warning — the info line tells you this is a best-effort derived pattern and asks you to verify it, which is the honest framing for a heuristic this shallow.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>A related limitation is that the generator is anchored to the <em>start</em> of each line and says nothing about how a line should end, how long the variable parts should be, or which characters are legal in them. Everything after the prefix is compressed into <code>\\d+</code>. That is exactly the information you have to supply yourself.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Generator", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Collect realistic samples.</strong> Gather a handful of real values of the type you want to match, not invented ones. Four to eight lines is usually enough to expose the common prefix; one or two lines will simply reflect whatever you happened to type.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Paste them one per line</strong> into the main input area. Line breaks are meaningful here: the tool splits on newlines, trims each line, and ignores blank lines entirely.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Run the transform.</strong> The output panel shows the derived pattern as a single string. If the input area was empty it returns a prompt asking you to paste sample lines rather than an error.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Read the info line.</strong> It carries the warning that the pattern is best-effort and should be verified before use.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Take the draft into the Regex Tester.</strong> Copy the pattern, switch to the tester, and run it against a <em>larger</em> sample than the one you fed the generator. Sharpen the variable sections — replace <code>\\d+</code> with a bounded quantifier such as <code>\\d{4}</code>, add an end anchor, tighten the allowed characters — until it matches what you want and rejects what you do not.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Bootstrapping a pattern for identifiers.</strong> Given a column of primary keys, invoice numbers or device serials, the generator gives you the fixed prefix and tells you whether a digit run is the variable part — which is the structure of most identifier formats.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Reverse-engineering an undocumented format.</strong> When a partner system hands you a file of codes with no specification, pasting a sample and reading back the common prefix is a fast way to discover that, for example, every record begins with the same two-letter feed code.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Writing a scraping or parsing seed.</strong> A first-draft pattern is easier to iterate on than a blank field, even when you expect to rewrite most of it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Sanity-checking a pattern someone else wrote.</strong> Feed the generator the strings the existing pattern is supposed to match. If the generator's draft and the existing pattern disagree about the common prefix, that difference is worth investigating.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Teaching the shape of a format.</strong> Because the derivation is transparent, the output is a compact way to state what a family of values has in common.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Paste four account references, one per line:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>acct_88213<br/>acct_90455<br/>acct_10977<br/>acct_33120</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Each line is trimmed at the first non-alphanumeric character, so <code>acct_88213</code> reduces to <code>acct</code>. All four reduce to the same value, giving a longest common prefix of <code>acct</code>. Every line contains a digit, so the digit rule fires. The output is <code>acct\\d+</code>, and the info line reminds you to verify it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now consider a weaker case. Paste <code>acct_88213</code> and <code>order_90455</code>. The trimmed values are <code>acct</code> and <code>order</code> with no shared prefix, so the prefix contributes nothing; both lines contain digits, so the output collapses to <code>\\d+</code> — a pattern that matches any digits anywhere and has lost all the structure your samples actually had. That is the heuristic telling you plainly that your samples do not share a shape worth deriving.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Finally, a mixed case that shows the prefix rule doing its job: lines <code>build-4471</code>, <code>build-4472</code> and <code>build-9901</code> trim to <code>build</code> and produce <code>build\\d+</code>. Tightening that to <code>^build\\d{4}$</code> in the Regex Tester turns a loose draft into an exact validator.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Do not expect prose input to work.</strong> The generator reads samples, not sentences. Typing <em>match an email address</em> produces a pattern derived from the characters in that sentence.</li><li><strong>Feed it the variety, not the average.</strong> If one line in twenty has a different prefix, include it. The common-prefix step shortens the pattern for every outlier you supply, which is how you discover that your data is not as uniform as you assumed.</li><li><strong>Treat <code>\\d+</code> as a placeholder, not an answer.</strong> It is unbounded by construction. Most real formats use a fixed-width numeric field, and <code>\\d{4}</code> or <code>\\d{6}</code> is what you actually want.</li><li><strong>Nothing is anchored.</strong> The emitted pattern has no <code>^</code> or <code>$</code>, so it will match your prefix in the middle of unrelated text. Add anchors as the first refinement.</li><li><strong>The trimmed prefix is case-sensitive.</strong> If your data mixes <code>Acct</code> and <code>acct</code>, the common prefix is empty. Decide deliberately whether you want the <code>i</code> flag.</li><li><strong>Always widen the verification sample.</strong> The generator has seen only what you pasted. A pattern that matches those exact lines proves nothing about the next thousand.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I describe what I want in plain English instead of pasting samples?</h3>Not with this tool. It derives patterns from example strings by finding their common prefix and detecting whether they contain digits. Natural-language descriptions require a different class of system entirely, and even then the output needs verification against real data." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why is my pattern missing the digits I can see in my samples?</h3>Because only the presence of a digit is detected, not its position or length. Everything downstream of the common prefix is represented by <code>\\d+</code>, so the literal digit runs you pasted are not reproduced individually." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why did I get <code>[\\w\\s]+</code>?</h3>That is the fallback, used when your lines share no alphanumeric prefix and not every line contains a digit. It is a catch-all rather than a pattern, and it signals that the samples are too heterogeneous to derive structure from — or that the values are better described by an enumeration than a pattern." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How many sample lines should I provide?</h3>Enough to include every shape your data takes. If every value follows one format, four to eight lines is plenty. If there are two or three formats — plain identifiers and ones with a suffix, say — include all of them so you can see the prefix collapse and know you need alternation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is the derived pattern safe to use in production as-is?</h3>No. It is unanchored, uses an unbounded digit quantifier, and was derived from a sample far too small to establish a validation rule. Treat it as a starting point, verify it in the Regex Tester, and add anchors and bounds before it governs any real input." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The generator's natural partner is the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>, which is where a draft pattern becomes a verified one. If you would rather assemble the pattern from understood pieces, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> documents the token types the syntax uses, and the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a> lists quantifiers, groups and character classes in one place. For common data shapes that already have well-tested recipes, start from the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a> instead of deriving from samples.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-generator", regex_generatorSeo);

const regex_cheatsheetSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Cheatsheet Is", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Cheatsheet is a <strong>printed reference for ECMAScript regex syntax</strong>, grouped into four tables: characters, quantifiers, groups, and character classes. It takes no input. Running it produces a fixed, aligned text block you can copy into a scratch file, a code comment header, a team wiki, or the notes app you keep open while writing a parser.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Its value is that it is a <em>complete, printable subset</em> rather than an exhaustive index. Every entry is a construct you will actually reach for in JavaScript: the six character escapes, the three bounded quantifier forms, the capture and non-capture group forms, lookaheads, and the basic class syntax. Nothing here requires a lookup elsewhere to use.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>It is also a deliberately bounded reference. It does not cover possessive quantifiers or atomic groups, because ECMAScript does not have them. It does not list <code>\\A</code> or <code>\\Z</code>, because they do not exist in this dialect. What remains is a sheet you can trust to be valid JavaScript — which is a genuinely useful property, since most cheat sheets in circulation are written against PCRE or Perl and quietly contain constructs that throw here.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Four Sections Work", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Characters</strong> covers the shorthand escapes and the two anchors. <code>.</code> is any character except a newline — unless the <code>s</code> flag is set, in which case it does cross line breaks. <code>\\d</code> and <code>\\D</code> are digit and non-digit, <code>\\w</code> and <code>\\W</code> are word and non-word, <code>\\s</code> and <code>\\S</code> are whitespace and non-whitespace. <code>\\b</code> and <code>\\B</code> are the boundary assertions, and <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> are the string anchors that become line anchors under the <code>m</code> flag.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Quantifiers</strong> is where most correctness bugs live. <code>a?</code> is zero or one, <code>a*</code> is zero or more, <code>a+</code> is one or more, <code>a{3}</code> is exactly three, <code>a{2,4}</code> is between two and four, and <code>a{2,}</code> is two or more. All of them are <em>greedy</em> by default: they consume as much as they can and then give back only as much as the rest of the pattern forces them to. Appending <code>?</code> makes one lazy, so <code>a+?</code> takes as little as possible. The <code>a{}</code> forms are worth preferring over stacked wildcards precisely because they bound the amount of text a pattern can swallow.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Groups</strong> lists the four forms you need in practice. <code>(...)</code> is a capturing group and its contents appear in the match result. <code>(?:...)</code> groups without capturing, which is what you want when you are only applying a quantifier to a sequence. <code>(?<name>...)</code> is a named capture, read back through the <code>groups</code> property. <code>(?=...)</code> is a lookahead that asserts what follows without consuming it, and <code>(?!...)</code> is its negation. Lookbehind — <code>(?<=...)</code> and <code>(?<!...)</code> — is supported in current browsers but is the newest of these constructs, so it is the one worth checking against your oldest supported engine.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Classes</strong> covers the bracket syntax. <code>[abc]</code> matches any one of the listed characters, <code>[^abc]</code> matches any character <em>not</em> listed, and <code>[a-z]</code> matches a range. Inside a class, most metacharacters lose their meaning and become literal — which is why <code>[.]</code> matches a full stop rather than any character, and why a hyphen must go first or last (or be escaped) unless it is meant as a range.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Cheatsheet", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Open the tool and trigger the output.</strong> There is no input area, because there is nothing to input. This tool generates rather than transforms, so it is not live — you press <strong>Transform</strong> to produce the sheet.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Read or copy the result.</strong> The output is plain text with the four section headings <code>## Characters</code>, <code>## Quantifiers</code>, <code>## Groups</code> and <code>## Classes</code>, each followed by aligned token and description columns. The alignment comes from padding, so it survives being pasted into a monospaced editor or a code comment.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Keep it where you write patterns.</strong> Because it contains no constructs that JavaScript rejects, the sheet is safe to paste into a repository as a comment block above a dense pattern, or into a wiki page your team consults during review.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Verify anything not on the sheet.</strong> If you reach for a construct that is absent — <code>\\p{...}</code> Unicode property escapes, lookbehind, backreferences — treat the absence as a prompt to confirm two things: that the construct exists in ECMAScript at all, and that it is supported in the browsers you target. The Regex Tester will tell you immediately whether the engine accepts it.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Code review reference.</strong> When reviewing a dense pattern you did not write, having the syntax table open next to it removes a lot of context-switching. It is particularly useful for confirming the difference between greedy and lazy quantifiers, which changes a pattern's meaning without changing its shape.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Documenting a non-obvious pattern.</strong> Pasting the relevant section above a complex expression gives the next reader the vocabulary to decode it. Explaining why a group is non-capturing, or why a class excludes a hyphen, is a one-line comment once the reference is in the file.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Learning the dialect you actually ship.</strong> If your regex exposure came from Python, PHP, grep or a general-purpose online tester, the sheet is a compact correction of the constructs that do not carry over. The absence of possessive quantifiers and atomic groups is as informative as the entries present.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Interview and teaching preparation.</strong> The four categories map closely to how regex questions are usually structured, making the sheet a reasonable revision outline.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>A portable offline reference.</strong> Copy the text once and you have the syntax available without a network connection, in any editor, on any machine.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example: Reading a Pattern Against the Sheet", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Take a realistic pattern from a validation routine:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>^(?<year>\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})$</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Walking it against the sheet: the leading <code>^</code> comes from the Characters section and anchors to the start of the subject. <code>(?<year>...)</code> comes from the Groups section — a named capture, read back as <code>match.groups.year</code>. Inside it, <code>\\d{4}</code> combines the <code>\\d</code> shorthand with the exact-count quantifier, so it requires precisely four digits. The literal hyphen is not on the sheet because literal characters need no entry. Two unnamed <code>(\\d{2})</code> groups follow, captured positionally as groups one and two. The trailing <code>$</code> anchors the end.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That read exposes the pattern's assumption. Because <code>\\d</code> accepts any digit and the quantifiers are exact counts, <code>2024-13-45</code> matches perfectly. The sheet tells you what the tokens do, not whether they express a valid date — that is a range problem, and it is why date validation by regex alone is a common source of permissive validators.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Remember that <code>\\d</code> is not <code>[0-9]</code> everywhere.</strong> In JavaScript without the <code>u</code> flag the two are equivalent, but the shorthand is tied to digit semantics rather than an ASCII range, which matters when you port a pattern between engines.</li><li><strong>Do not confuse greedy with lazy.</strong> <code><.+></code> against <code><a><b></code> matches the whole string; <code><.+?></code> matches <code><a></code>. The sheet lists both forms, and choosing the wrong one is the single most common cause of over-matching.</li><li><strong>Anchor your validators.</strong> An unanchored pattern is a <em>search</em>. If you mean to validate a whole field, wrap it in <code>^</code> and <code>$</code>, otherwise <code>abc</code> will happily pass <code>xxabcxx</code>.</li><li><strong>Prefer non-capturing groups when you only need grouping.</strong> <code>(?:...)</code> keeps the match result clean and avoids shifting the indices of the groups you do care about.</li><li><strong>A character class is a set of single characters.</strong> <code>[abc]</code> matches one character, not the string. Sequences belong in a group, not a class.</li><li><strong>Escape the hyphen inside a class.</strong> <code>[a-z-0-9]</code> is read as ranges; put the hyphen first or last, or escape it, to make it literal.</li><li><strong>Check lookbehind against your oldest target.</strong> It is supported in current engines but is the newest construct on this sheet, so it is the likeliest to fail in older Safari or an embedded JS runtime.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does this cheatsheet not include possessive quantifiers or atomic groups?</h3>Because ECMAScript does not implement them. They exist in PCRE, Java and .NET, and a pattern using <code>a++</code> or <code>(?>...)</code> will throw here. Excluding them keeps every entry on the sheet valid in the browser engine you are actually shipping to." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is the cheatsheet interactive or searchable?</h3>No. It is a fixed reference that you generate and copy. If you want to search for a recipe rather than a token — an email pattern, a URL pattern, an IPv4 pattern — use <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a>, which filters a library of ready-made patterns by name." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>What is the difference between the Characters and Classes sections?</h3>Characters lists the shorthand escapes that each match a whole category of single characters — <code>\\d</code>, <code>\\w</code>, <code>\\s</code> and their negations, plus the anchors. Classes is the explicit bracket syntax for constructing your own set, such as <code>[a-z0-9_]</code>. The two overlap in meaning, and <code>\\w</code> is close to <code>[A-Za-z0-9_]</code>, but the shorthand is fixed while a class is whatever you define." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I use these tokens in Python or PHP?</h3>Most of the shorthands, quantifiers and class syntax are shared, but the dialect is not identical. Named groups use different syntax in Python (<code>(?P<name>...)</code>), and the PCRE-only constructs deliberately absent from this sheet are available there. Treat this as a JavaScript reference and check ported patterns in their target engine." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>To test a pattern built from these tokens, use the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>, which reports matches and capture groups. To see which of these constructs a specific pattern contains, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> scans it and lists the token types it recognises — including the character-class and quantifier forms described above. For complete patterns rather than individual tokens, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a> returns recipes for common data shapes, and the <a href=\"/developers/regex-generator\">Regex Generator</a> drafts a pattern from sample values when you have examples but no pattern yet.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-cheatsheet", regex_cheatsheetSeo);

const regex_explainerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Explainer Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Explainer reads a pattern and tells you <strong>which regex token types it contains</strong>, in plain English. Paste something like <code>^(?<year>\\d{4})</code> and it comes back with a short list: start of string, capture or non-capture group, digit class, repetition quantifier.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>It is most useful in two situations. The first is a pattern you did not write — inherited code, a configuration file, a rule inside a validation library — where you want a fast inventory of what the expression is built from before deciding whether it does what its name claims. The second is your own pattern after a long debugging session, when you have edited it so many times that you no longer trust your reading of it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>One thing needs to be said plainly, because it shapes how you should use the output. This tool is a <strong>token scanner, not a parser</strong>. It reports which recognised token types appear in your pattern, deduplicated — not where each one is, not how they nest, and not what any group actually captures. Reading its output as a structural explanation of the pattern will mislead you. Reading it as a checklist of constructs present is accurate and genuinely helpful.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Regex Explainer Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The implementation is a fixed table of token patterns, each paired with a description. For every entry in the table it asks whether that token appears anywhere in your pattern; if it does, the description is added to a set of findings. The set is what removes duplicates, which is why a pattern containing five digit classes still reports <code>Digit [0-9]</code> exactly once.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The table covers the constructs you meet most often in everyday JavaScript work: escape sequences for digits, word characters, whitespace and their negations; the word-boundary assertions; the three quantifier characters; the literals-matched escapes for a dot and a backslash; the anchors; alternation; the capture and non-capture group openers; a generic character-class pattern; and the bounded repetition form.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Two details of the scan are worth knowing because they explain output that otherwise looks wrong. The bounded-repetition entry matches <code>{3}</code> and <code>{2,4}</code> — an exact count or a closed range — but <strong>not</strong> the open-ended <code>{2,}</code> form, because that pattern requires a digit after the comma. And because the checks are independent rather than sequential, a construct can trigger more than one description; a pattern containing <code>\\.</code> reports both the escaped-literal-dot entry and the generic any-character entry, since both patterns are present in the text.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The tool does not validate your pattern. A malformed expression still gets scanned and still returns a token list. Validation happens when the engine tries to compile it, which is what the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> does — if you need to know whether a pattern is syntactically legal, test it rather than explain it.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Explainer", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Put the pattern in the input area.</strong> The main textarea takes the <em>pattern</em>, not a subject string — the opposite of the Regex Tester, where the textarea holds the text being searched. This is the most common way to get a surprising result from this tool.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Run the transform.</strong> There are no options and no flags to set. The explainer describes the syntax of what you pasted; it does not evaluate anything.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Read the header line.</strong> The output opens by echoing the pattern back as <code>Pattern: ...</code>, which is a useful confirmation that the whole expression arrived intact — a truncated paste is easy to miss in a long pattern.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Read the findings list.</strong> Under a <code>Tokens found:</code> heading, each recognised construct is listed once as a bullet with its meaning. If nothing in the table matched, the output says the pattern was treated as a literal — an important signal that either the pattern is genuinely plain text or that it uses constructs outside the table.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Treat the list as an inventory, then verify separately.</strong> Copy the findings if they are useful for a code comment, and confirm actual behaviour by running the pattern in the Regex Tester.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Auditing a pattern during code review.</strong> Before approving a validation change, confirm that the new expression really uses the constructs it appears to. A pattern that looks anchored but contains no anchor entry is a finding.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Preparing a pattern for documentation.</strong> The token list is a reasonable starting point for the one-line comment above a dense expression, particularly when the pattern was written by someone who has since left the team.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Checking a ported pattern's assumptions.</strong> When an expression arrives from a Python or PHP codebase, the inventory shows which JavaScript constructs it relies on — and its absence of entries for constructs the table does not track then tells you exactly which parts still need manual verification.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Teaching and onboarding.</strong> For someone new to regex, seeing <code>\\d+</code> described as a digit class plus a one-or-more quantifier is a concrete, immediate explanation of two ideas at once.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Spotting the literal-pattern mistake.</strong> If a pattern you expected to be complex reports as plain literal text, you have almost certainly pasted a string that already lost its escapes somewhere upstream — through a JSON layer, a shell heredoc, or a chat client.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Paste this into the input area:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>^\\s*(?<key>[\\w-]+)\\s*=\\s*(.+)$</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>This is a plausible-looking configuration-file line pattern. The explainer echoes it back, then reports findings along these lines: start and end anchors, whitespace and non-whitespace classes, a word character and a character class, a capture or non-capture group, the any-character entry, and the one-or-more and zero-or-more quantifiers.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now read those findings critically. <code>.+</code> paired with <code>$</code> will consume the rest of the line greedily — the inventory told you the any-character entry is present, which is the cue to ask whether that is intended. More importantly, the word-character entry being present is the cue to notice that <code>\\w-</code> inside a class means <em>word characters or a literal hyphen</em>, and that the trailing <code>\\s*=\\s*</code> tolerates whitespace you may not want to accept. The token list did not tell you any of that; it told you where to look.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Notice too that the pattern is dense with escapes, and that the output does not attempt to reproduce their positions. That is the scanner-versus-parser distinction in practice: you get the vocabulary of the pattern, not its grammar.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Do not paste subject text by mistake.</strong> This tool scans a pattern. If you paste a paragraph of log lines, you will get a list describing whichever characters happen to look like tokens, which is meaningless.</li><li><strong>The list is deduplicated, so it is not a count.</strong> Three quantifiers and one quantifier produce identical output. Never use the findings to reason about how many times something occurs in a pattern.</li><li><strong>Positional and nesting information is absent by design.</strong> The output cannot tell you which group a quantifier applies to, or whether an anchor is inside an alternation. For those questions, read the pattern directly.</li><li><strong>Watch for the literal fallback.</strong> A <em>treated as literal</em> message on a pattern you know is complex almost always means the escapes were stripped in transit. Check for double-escaping before assuming the tool is wrong.</li><li><strong>Open-ended repetition is not reported.</strong> <code>{2,}</code> produces no repetition finding even though <code>{2}</code> and <code>{2,4}</code> both do. Treat that gap as expected rather than as evidence of a problem.</li><li><strong>Absence of a finding is not proof of absence.</strong> Lookbehind, backreferences, Unicode property escapes and inline flags are simply not in the table. Their absence from the output says nothing about the pattern.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does the explanation not match the order of my pattern?</h3>Because it is not an ordered walk through the expression. Each token type is tested independently against the whole pattern and reported at most once, so the output is a set of constructs found rather than a left-to-right reading. Sequence and nesting are deliberately out of scope." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can the explainer tell me what my capture groups captured?</h3>No. It describes syntax, not matching. To see actual captured values you need to run the pattern against input in the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>, which prints the group array alongside each match." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does it check whether my pattern is valid?</h3>It does not validate at all — an unbalanced parenthesis still returns a token list. The engine only complains when it compiles the pattern, which happens in the Regex Tester. Use that tool whenever syntactic legality is the question." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why did it say my pattern was treated as literal when it clearly contains metacharacters?</h3>Usually because the metacharacters did not survive the trip. If a pattern is pasted through a JSON string, a shell command or a templating layer, backslashes are frequently consumed and <code>\\d+</code> arrives as <code>d+</code>. The explainer sees plain characters and says so." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the output cover every regex construct?</h3>No. It covers a practical subset of common constructs. Lookbehind, backreferences, Unicode property escapes such as <code>\\p{...}</code>, named-group syntax and inline flags are outside its table, so they will not appear in the findings even when present in your pattern." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The explainer's natural companion is the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>, which answers the behavioural question the explainer leaves open: what does this pattern actually match, and what do its groups capture? To look up the meaning of a construct the scanner did not recognise, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a> covers the core syntax, and if you need a pattern rather than an explanation, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-generator\">Regex Generator</a> drafts one from sample values.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-explainer", regex_explainerSeo);

const regex_replace_testerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Replace Tester Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Replace Tester shows you <strong>the result of a find-and-replace operation before you commit it</strong>. You supply a subject, a pattern, and a replacement string; it runs the substitution and returns the transformed text, together with a length delta showing how many characters you gained or lost.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The problem it solves is specific. Find-and-replace against real data is not reversible in the way people assume. A pattern that is slightly too broad will quietly rewrite lines you never intended to touch, and by the time you notice, the original is gone — or worse, it is a migration script that already ran against a production column. Running the substitution against a representative sample first is the cheapest possible insurance.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>It is equally useful for building the replacement string itself. Replacement templates have their own syntax for referring to captured text, and that syntax is where most silent failures happen: a template that inserts nothing, or inserts a literal dollar sign where a value was expected, produces output that looks plausible and is wrong.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Regex Replace Tester Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The tool compiles your pattern with the flags you selected and hands it, along with the replacement string, to JavaScript's own <code>String.prototype.replace</code>. That means you are exercising the exact semantics your application code will use — no reimplementation, no dialect drift.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The single most important consequence of using the native method is how the <code>g</code> flag changes the operation. <strong>Without <code>g</code>, only the first match is replaced.</strong> With it, every match is replaced. The default here is <code>g</code>, which matches the behaviour most people expect from a replace utility, but it also means that toggling <code>g</code> off is how you get first-match-only behaviour — a distinction that matters enormously when you are normalising a file rather than editing one line.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Replacement strings are templates, not literal text. The engine interprets several sequences: <code>$&</code> for the whole matched substring, <code>$1</code> through <code>$99</code> for numbered capture groups, <code>$<name></code> for a named group, <code>$\u0060</code> for the text preceding the match, <code>$'</code> for the text following it, and <code>$$</code> for a literal dollar sign.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That last point catches people repeatedly. A replacement intended to emit a currency amount must write <code>$$</code> to produce one <code>$</code>. Written as a single <code>$</code> followed by a digit it is read as a group reference — and if that group does not exist in the pattern, the engine substitutes an empty string instead of raising an error. You get a plausible result, a wrong one, and no diagnostic. This is precisely the class of mistake the length-delta readout is designed to expose: if you expected ten characters added and the counter reports a small negative number, the template did not do what you thought.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>An invalid pattern does not produce output; the constructor throws and the error surfaces instead. The tool deliberately offers no inline diff, so the length delta and the transformed text are your two signals. When the substitution is subtle, verify it against a diff tool rather than reading the output line by line.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Replace Tester", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Load the subject text.</strong> Paste the content you want to transform into the main input area. Always use a realistic sample that includes at least one line you expect to match and one you expect to be left alone — a sample where everything matches proves nothing about the pattern's precision.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Set the pattern.</strong> In the options bar, the first field is labelled <strong>Pattern</strong> (placeholder <code>pattern</code>). Enter the pattern body without slashes or flags.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Set the replacement.</strong> The second field is labelled <strong>Replace</strong> (placeholder <code>replacement</code>). This field takes a replacement template, so <code>$1</code> and <code>$&</code> refer to captured text rather than literal characters.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Confirm the flags.</strong> The <strong>Flags</strong> row contains the six toggles <code>g</code>, <code>i</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>u</code>, <code>y</code>, with <code>g</code> on by default. Turn <code>g</code> off to replace only the first occurrence.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Read the output and the length delta.</strong> The output panel holds the full transformed text. The info line reports the change as original-to-result character counts. A figure that is wildly off from your mental estimate is the fastest signal that the pattern matched more, or less, than you intended.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 6 — Copy the result.</strong> The transformation is not applied to your input in place, so take the output via the copy control and treat the original as untouched.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Pre-flight a data migration.</strong> Renaming columns, renumbering records or rewriting identifiers is exactly the operation you want to rehearse on a sample. Because the length delta reports the aggregate change, a script that silently rewrote twice as many rows as expected shows up immediately.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Redact sensitive values before sharing.</strong> Replace email addresses, tokens or account numbers in a log excerpt with placeholders, and confirm from the output that nothing recognisable survived. Building the replacement template here is far safer than editing by hand.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Reshape captured groups into a new format.</strong> When a structured value needs to be reassembled differently — a date reordered, a name split, a log line converted to CSV columns — the replacement template does the work and the output shows the result directly.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Debug a replacement that is inserting nothing.</strong> An empty substitution where a group reference was expected is almost always a nonexistent group number or an unescaped dollar sign. Testing the template here isolates the cause in seconds.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Suppose you want to rewrite a log prefix into a cleaner form. Paste this subject:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>INFO 2024-03-11 request 8821 completed<br/>ERROR 2024-03-11 request 8822 failed</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Set <strong>Pattern</strong> to <code>(\\w+) (\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}) request (\\d+)</code> and <strong>Replace</strong> to <code>[$1] $2 req:$3</code>, leaving <code>g</code> active. The output becomes:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>[INFO] 2024-03-11 req:8821 completed<br/>[ERROR] 2024-03-11 req:8822 failed</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Every template reference did real work: <code>$1</code> took the level, <code>$2</code> the date, <code>$3</code> the request number. Because <code>g</code> was on, both lines were rewritten.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now the instructive failure. Change <strong>Replace</strong> to <code>[$4] $2</code>. Group four does not exist, so the engine substitutes nothing and the level annotation vanishes — no error, no warning. The output still looks like a plausible log line minus its severity, which is exactly why an unreviewed replacement template is dangerous. Watching the character delta is what turns that from a silent corruption into an obvious one.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>A second variant shows the effect of the <code>g</code> toggle. With <code>g</code> off and the pattern unchanged, only the first line is rewritten and the second is returned untouched. If your intent was a single targeted edit, that is the correct configuration — but you must say so deliberately rather than by leaving the default in place.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>An unescaped dollar sign is the most common silent bug.</strong> To emit a literal <code>$</code> write <code>$$</code>. A single <code>$</code> before a digit is read as a group reference, and a reference to a nonexistent group expands to nothing rather than throwing.</li><li><strong>Remember that <code>g</code> is on by default.</strong> The tool and most replace utilities default to replacing every match. If you want a single edit, toggle it off — do not assume the first match.</li><li><strong>Test with negative cases.</strong> Include a line in your sample that should <em>not</em> match. A pattern proven only against matching input is untested.</li><li><strong>Watch the length delta.</strong> It is your only diff signal. A change far from your estimate means the pattern matched more than you intended, often because a quantifier is greedy or an anchor is missing.</li><li><strong>Check whether your pattern needs anchors.</strong> An unanchored pattern will rewrite matches embedded inside larger tokens, which is rarely what a field-level reformat wants.</li><li><strong>Do not rely on the replacement to validate.</strong> The template cannot reject input; it can only reshape whatever the pattern matched. Validate first, then transform.</li><li><strong>Beware of replacing into invalidity.</strong> A substitution can produce syntactically broken output — malformed JSON, an unbalanced quote, a duplicate key — even when the pattern matched correctly. Re-parse the result when the target is a structured format.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why was only the first occurrence replaced?</h3>Because the <code>g</code> flag is off. <code>String.prototype.replace</code> replaces only the first match unless the regex is global. The flags row shows the current state — toggle <code>g</code> on to rewrite every match." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How do I insert a literal dollar sign in the replacement?</h3>Write <code>$$</code>. The replacement string is a template, so a lone <code>$</code> that precedes a digit or a bracket is interpreted as a group reference rather than a currency symbol." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why did my replacement insert nothing at all?</h3>Almost always one of two causes: the template references a group number the pattern does not define, or a dollar sign was meant literally and was read as a reference. Both expand to an empty string instead of raising an error, so the failure is silent — check the group count in the pattern, and confirm the template's references against it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I use named groups in the replacement?</h3>Yes, if your pattern defines them. Write the group as <code>(?<name>...)</code> in the pattern and refer to it as <code>$<name></code> in the replacement. Named references are more readable than positional ones and survive pattern edits that reorder groups." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is my input modified or sent anywhere?</h3>Neither. The substitution runs in the page using the browser's own regex engine, and your subject text is not altered in place — you copy the result out. Nothing is uploaded." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Before replacing, confirm what your pattern matches with the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> — it reports each match and its capture groups, which tells you the group numbers your replacement template can safely reference. To review the result of a subtle substitution, the <a href=\"/developers/diff-checker\">Diff Checker</a> shows the changed regions directly. If you need to understand a pattern's components rather than its effect, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> lists the token types it recognises.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-replace-tester", regex_replace_testerSeo);

const regex_librarySeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Recipe Library Contains", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>This tool returns <strong>ready-made regular expressions for six common kinds of data</strong>: email addresses, URLs, IPv4 addresses, phone numbers, hexadecimal colours and ISO dates. You search by name, copy the pattern, and adapt it.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The recipes are worth understanding as a set, because they share a design philosophy and therefore share a failure mode. Each one is written to <em>find</em> matches inside a larger body of text rather than to accept or reject a single field. None is anchored, several use deliberately permissive character classes, and none performs range or calendar validation. That makes them excellent for extraction and unreliable as validators, and this page documents exactly where each one needs hardening before it can guard anything.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "When to Use These Recipes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Scanning unstructured documents.</strong> Pulling email addresses, URLs or IP addresses out of prose, log output or pasted email bodies is exactly what permissive, unanchored patterns are good at. You want recall at this stage and you will filter the candidates afterwards.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Redacting before you share.</strong> Finding candidate phone numbers or addresses so they can be replaced with placeholders is a place where over-matching is harmless and under-matching is a disclosure risk.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Getting a testable baseline fast.</strong> A pattern that over-matches is easier to tighten than an empty field is to fill, particularly for formats whose edge cases are well understood.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Comparing an implementation against a reference shape.</strong> When a specification says ISO date or hex colour, a conventional pattern gives reviewers something concrete to measure the code against.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Not for enforcement.</strong> If a match will decide whether a user may proceed — a sign-up form, an API boundary, a migration gate — a permissive recipe is the wrong basis. Tighten it first, and where the rule involves ranges or arithmetic, move the check into ordinary code.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Recipe Lookup Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The lookup is a case-insensitive substring filter over a fixed library of six recipes. Typing <code>em</code> returns the email recipe; typing <code>date</code> returns the ISO date recipe; submitting nothing returns all six. Matching happens against the recipe <em>name</em>, not the pattern text, so searching for <code>\\d</code> returns no results even though digits appear in several patterns — search by what the pattern is for, not by what it contains.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The six recipes and their exact bodies are: <strong>email</strong> <code>[^@]+@[^@]+\\.[^@]+</code>, <strong>url</strong> <code>https?://[^\\s]+</code>, <strong>ipv4</strong> <code>\\b(?:\\d{1,3}\\.){3}\\d{1,3}\\b</code>, <strong>phone</strong> <code>\\+?\\d[\\d\\s\\-()]{7,}</code>, <strong>hex color</strong> <code>#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}</code>, and <strong>date iso</strong> <code>\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}</code>. Each is a pragmatic starting point rather than a validator, and the next section explains what that means for each one.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>A note on scope, since it affects which of our tools you should reach for. This tool and <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a> currently expose the same six recipes through the same lookup, so they will return identical results for identical queries. Treat this page as the reference for adapting a recipe to your own constraints, and if you only need to retrieve a pattern either page will do.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes: Hardening Each Recipe", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Email.</strong> The recipe requires at least one character before and after the <code>@</code>, and at least one dot in the domain. It does not restrict which characters those may be, so it happily matches constructions with spaces, and it will report matches inside longer text because nothing is anchored. It also rejects quoted local parts, which are legal in the specification.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>URL.</strong> The pattern requires a scheme and then consumes everything up to the next whitespace, greedily. On a line of prose that means it swallows trailing punctuation — a closing parenthesis or a full stop at the end of a sentence becomes part of the match. Strip trailing punctuation in code after matching, or tighten the class. It also will not match protocol-relative or bare-host forms, which is usually the right call but should be deliberate.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>IPv4.</strong> This is the classic gap. <code>\\d{1,3}</code> accepts one to three digits without constraining their value, so the recipe matches <code>999.999.999.999</code>, which is not a valid address. If you need range-accurate validation, each octet must be expressed as an alternation covering <code>0-199</code>, <code>200-249</code> and <code>250-255</code>, which makes the pattern markedly longer — and is often better handled by parsing and testing each octet numerically.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Phone.</strong> The recipe is intentionally permissive: an optional leading plus, then a digit, then at least seven more characters drawn from digits, spaces, hyphens and parentheses. It will therefore match any long run of digits in unstructured text, including an order number or a timestamp. It carries no country semantics. Use it to <em>find</em> candidate phone numbers in a document, then normalise and validate separately — never as a sign-up gate.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Hex colour.</strong> The quantifier <code>{3,8}</code> accepts every length from three to eight, but the CSS syntax only defines four valid lengths: three, four, six and eight digits. The recipe therefore matches five- and seven-digit strings that no browser would honour. Pin the alternation to the legal lengths if correctness matters.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>ISO date.</strong> The shape is enforced, the calendar is not. <code>2024-13-45</code> matches. Pair it with numeric range checks on the month and day, and decide explicitly whether you are willing to accept edge cases like <code>2024-02-30</code>.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using a Recipe", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Search by purpose.</strong> Type the kind of data you are looking for — <code>email</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>ipv4</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>hex</code>, <code>date</code> — into the input area. Submitting an empty query lists all six recipes so you can see the full set first.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Copy the pattern.</strong> The result is returned as a name and pattern pair. Copy the pattern body without the name prefix; the lookup output pairs them with a separator for readability.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Test it against your own data.</strong> Paste the pattern into the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> along with real samples. This step is not optional — a recipe tested only against the examples on this page has not been tested against your data at all.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Add anchors if you are validating.</strong> Every recipe here is written to <em>find</em> matches, so none is anchored. For field validation, wrap the pattern in <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> so that extra characters around a valid value cause a rejection rather than a partial match.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Harden deliberately.</strong> Tighten the specific weakness documented above for the recipe you chose, rather than trusting the permissive default in a validation path.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example: Hardening the IPv4 Recipe", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Suppose you are parsing server logs and need to pull out addresses. Search for <code>ipv4</code> and you get <code>\\b(?:\\d{1,3}\\.){3}\\d{1,3}\\b</code>. Read it as a sentence: a word boundary, then a non-capturing group matching one to three digits followed by a dot, repeated exactly three times, then one to three more digits, then a word boundary. The group is non-capturing because the repetition is the point and you do not want three redundant groups in the result.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Run it in the Regex Tester against <code>10.0.0.7 and 999.999.999.999</code>. Both match. The recipe is behaving exactly as written — <code>\\d{1,3}</code> limits how many digits an octet may contain, not which numbers it may represent — and for log parsing that is usually fine, because a four-part dotted string with octet-like widths is overwhelmingly likely to be an address.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now suppose the same pattern is about to validate a form field. The identical behaviour is now a defect: <code>999.999.999.999</code> is not a valid address and must be rejected. Constraining each octet to 0–255 requires an alternation covering <code>0-199</code>, <code>200-249</code> and <code>250-255</code>, which turns a readable pattern into a dense one that reviewers will struggle to check.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The better answer is to change the tool rather than the pattern. Use the recipe to <em>find</em> candidate strings, then split on dots and test each octet numerically with a bounds check. A handful of lines of ordinary code is easier to review, impossible to get subtly wrong, and immune to the range-encoding bugs that plague hand-written octet alternations. That is the general lesson these six recipes illustrate: regex is excellent at finding structured text and a poor substitute for arithmetic or calendar logic.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does searching for a pattern fragment return nothing?</h3>Because the lookup filters recipe names rather than pattern contents. Search for <code>hex</code> or <code>url</code>, not for <code>#[0-9a-f]</code>. Only six named recipes exist, so a query that does not appear in any name returns no results." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is the IPv4 pattern safe for validating addresses?</h3>No. It constrains the number of digits per octet but not their value, so it accepts addresses such as <code>999.999.999.999</code>. It is a useful pattern for <em>finding</em> address-shaped strings; for validation, check each octet numerically between 0 and 255." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Will the email pattern reject invalid addresses?</h3>It rejects only the most obviously malformed shapes. Because the character classes are negations of <code>@</code>, it permits spaces and consecutive dots, and it is unanchored, so it will match an address embedded in a larger string. Treat a match as a lead, not as validation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How does this differ from Regex Cheatsheet Search?</h3>Operationally it does not — both filter the same six recipes with the same lookup, so identical queries return identical results. This page documents what each recipe guarantees and where it needs hardening; that one is the quick retrieval route. If you need a wider set of patterns, neither tool has it." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Whatever recipe you take from here should be confirmed in the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> against your own data before it governs anything. To rewrite matched values rather than find them, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-replace-tester\">Regex Replace Tester</a> applies a substitution with a replacement template and reports the length change. For the individual tokens used inside these patterns, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a> covers quantifiers, groups and character classes.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-library", regex_librarySeo);

const regex_visualizerSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Regex Visualizer Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The Regex Visualizer takes a pattern and produces a <strong>line-by-line character breakdown</strong>. It first checks that the pattern compiles, then walks the expression one character at a time, naming the role of each character it recognises and labelling the rest as literals.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>That makes it a useful first stop for a pattern whose meaning you cannot hold in your head — the dense validators that accumulate in codebases over years, the ones with four nested groups and three quantifiers where you can no longer tell by looking which <code>?</code> is a lazy modifier and which is an optional character.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>It is important to be precise about what this produces, because the shape of the output invites a wrong reading. This is <strong>not a parse tree and not a flow diagram</strong>. It is a character map. The distinction matters constantly in practice: a two-character token like <code>\\d</code> is processed as a backslash and then a <code>d</code>, so the breakdown reports the backslash as an escape prefix and the <code>d</code> as a literal. Multi-character constructs — <code>\\w</code>, <code>\\s</code>, <code>\\b</code>, <code>(?:</code>, <code>{2,4}</code> — are all decomposed the same way. The tool tells you where the special characters are; assembling them into tokens is still your job.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Regex Visualizer Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>There are two stages, and the first is a gate you will meet often.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Validation first.</strong> Before any breakdown, the tool passes your pattern to <code>new RegExp</code>. If the engine rejects it, you get the engine's own message prefixed with <code>Invalid regex:</code> and no character map at all. This is genuinely valuable on its own — it means a breakdown appearing in the output is positive evidence that the pattern is syntactically legal ECMAScript, which is more than a plain chart can tell you.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Then the per-character pass.</strong> The pattern is split into individual characters and each is looked up in a small table of meanings. Nine characters have entries: <code>^</code> is described as start of line, <code>$</code> as end of line, <code>.</code> as any character, <code>*</code> as zero or more times, <code>+</code> as one or more times, <code>?</code> as optional, <code>|</code> as alternation, and <code>\\</code> as an escape for the next character. Everything else — every letter, digit, bracket, brace and parenthesis — is reported as a literal.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The output is prefixed with the pattern echoed back as <code>Pattern: ...</code>, then one line per character with the character and its description separated by a tab. Because a tab is used rather than padding, the columns align in an editor or terminal but not in a proportional font.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Two consequences of the fixed table are worth internalising before you rely on the output. Characters with real structural meaning are reported as literals because they are not in the table: parentheses, square brackets, curly braces and the question mark inside a group all appear as plain literals even when they control grouping, classes or quantifiers. And the <code>?</code> entry is a single meaning — <em>optional</em> — even though the same character also makes a quantifier lazy when it follows one, and opens a non-capturing group when it follows an opening parenthesis. One character, three jobs, one description.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Regex Visualizer", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Paste the pattern into the input area.</strong> The textarea takes the pattern itself, not sample text. Surrounding whitespace is trimmed before validation, so a stray newline will not cause a spurious error — but a pattern wrapping across lines internally will.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Run the transform.</strong> There are no flags to set. If the pattern is malformed the output is an error message and nothing else, which is your first signal that the expression will not compile where it is going.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Confirm the echo line.</strong> The <code>Pattern:</code> header is a quick integrity check on a long paste. If it is truncated, everything downstream is describing something else.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Read the character list critically.</strong> Use the <em>escape</em> entries to locate the start of escape sequences, and the quantifier entries to spot where repetition is applied. Read the <em>literal</em> labels with suspicion on anything that is not actually alphanumeric — a bracket or brace reported as literal is an artefact of the character-by-character approach, not a statement about the pattern.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Cross-check with the token scanner.</strong> Because this tool splits tokens that the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> recognises whole, running both gives you the two halves: where the special characters sit, and which multi-character constructs are present.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Decoding an inherited pattern.</strong> When you need to change a validator nobody on the team remembers writing, a character map is a fast way to find every point where the pattern branches or repeats before you start editing.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Locating an escape-sequence bug.</strong> Double-escaping bugs are hard to see by eye, because <code>\\\\d</code> and <code>\\d</code> differ by one character. Mapping each character makes the escape characters visible in sequence, which is often enough to spot an extra level of escaping introduced by a JSON layer or a shell heredoc.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Checking that a pattern compiles before shipping it.</strong> The validation gate is a fast syntactic gate for a pattern assembled by string concatenation, where a missing bracket is easy to introduce and hard to notice.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Confirming which quantifier you actually typed.</strong> In expressions with several repetitions, the map shows every <code>*</code>, <code>+</code> and <code>?</code> in order of appearance, which makes an accidental extra quantifier obvious.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Paste a small but realistic pattern:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>^\\d{3}-\\d+$</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The validator accepts it, so the output opens with the echo line and a nine-line character map. Reading it as the tool produces it, <code>^</code> is reported as start of line, the backslash as escaping the next character, <code>d</code> as a literal, <code>{</code> as a literal, <code>3</code> as a literal, <code>}</code> as a literal, the hyphen as a literal, another backslash-d pair, <code>+</code> as one or more times, and <code>$</code> as end of line.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Notice what that output gets right and what it leaves to you. It correctly identified the anchors and the <code>+</code>, and it correctly showed that two escape characters are present. It did <em>not</em> tell you that <code>\\d</code> means a digit, that <code>{3}</code> is an exact-count repetition, or that <code>{</code> and <code>}</code> are a pair. Those are the multi-character tokens the character-by-character pass splits apart — which is exactly the gap the Regex Explainer fills, since its table recognises <code>\\d</code> and the bounded repetition form as single constructs.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now the negative case, which is the more instructive one. Paste <code>(?:abc)+</code>. The map reports <code>(</code> as a literal, <code>?</code> as optional, <code>:</code> as a literal, then each letter as a literal, then <code>)</code> as a literal and <code>+</code> as one or more times. Every word of that is misleading if taken at face value: there is nothing optional in this pattern, and the parentheses are not literals. The output is correct as a character inventory and wrong as a description of the expression — which is the single most important thing to understand about this tool.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Never read a multi-character token from the character map.</strong> <code>\\d</code>, <code>\\w</code>, <code>\\b</code> and <code>{2,4}</code> are all split. Use the Regex Explainer for tokens and this tool for character positions.</li><li><strong>Treat <em>literal</em> as unknown, not as literal.</strong> Brackets, braces, parentheses and colons are reported as literals because they are absent from the description table, not because the pattern uses them literally.</li><li><strong><code>?</code> has three meanings and one label.</strong> It makes a quantifier lazy, it marks a character as optional, and it opens a non-capturing or lookaround group. The map always says <em>optional</em>.</li><li><strong>The <code>.</code> description omits the <code>s</code>-flag caveat.</strong> Dot matches any character except a newline unless the <code>s</code> flag is set, and this tool describes neither case.</li><li><strong>Use the validation gate deliberately.</strong> A map appearing at all tells you the pattern compiled. If you only wanted that answer, you have it — and the Regex Tester will tell you the same thing while also showing what the pattern matches.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does the visualizer say my character class brackets are literals?</h3>Because the breakdown works character by character against a small table that covers nine special characters. Square brackets are not in that table, so they are labelled as literals even when they delimit a character class. The label means <em>not described here</em>, not <em>matches a bracket character</em>." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does this tool draw a diagram of the pattern?</h3>No. The output is plain aligned text — one line per character with a description next to it. There is no graphical rendering, no node graph and no matching-flow visualisation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why does <code>\\d</code> appear as two separate entries?</h3>Because the pass splits the pattern into individual characters. The backslash is reported as escaping the next character and the <code>d</code> as a literal. The same applies to every escape sequence and every multi-character construct, including bounded quantifiers." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can the visualizer tell me whether my pattern matches anything?</h3>No. It neither matches nor requires any input text — it only inspects the pattern's syntax and checks that it compiles. To see matches and captured groups, use the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How do I get a proper token-level explanation instead?</h3>Use the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a>. It scans for whole constructs — digit classes, word boundaries, quantifiers, groups, character classes — and describes them as units, which is the level of description most people are looking for when they open a visualiser." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Pair this tool with the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a>: the explainer recognises whole tokens such as <code>\\d</code> and <code>{2,4}</code> that this one splits into characters, so together they cover both token types present and character positions. To see what a pattern actually matches and what its groups capture, use the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a>, and for the meaning of a construct that neither scanner described, consult the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a>.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-visualizer", regex_visualizerSeo);

const regex_cheatsheet_searchSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What Regex Cheatsheet Search Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Regex Cheatsheet Search is a <strong>lookup for ready-made patterns</strong>. Instead of building an expression from syntax tokens, you name the kind of data you are looking for and get back a working pattern you can copy into your code.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>This is the right tool at the moment when you know <em>what</em> you need to match but not <em>how</em> to express it, and when the shape in question is common enough that someone has already worked out the trade-offs. The library holds six recipes covering the data types that dominate real validation code: email addresses, URLs, IPv4 addresses, phone numbers, hexadecimal colours and ISO dates.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Be clear about the level of the tool. This is not a syntax reference — that is the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a>, which lists quantifiers, groups and character classes. This is a catalogue of finished patterns, and its most valuable property is also its most dangerous one: every recipe is deliberately permissive, because it is built to <em>find</em> matches in text rather than to accept or reject a form field.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Search Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The mechanism is deliberately simple and worth knowing precisely, because it explains nearly every surprising result.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Your query is lowercased and trimmed, then compared against each recipe's <strong>name</strong> as a case-insensitive substring test. A query is a match if the recipe name contains it anywhere — so <code>em</code> returns the email recipe, <code>date</code> returns the ISO date recipe, and <code>url</code> returns the URL recipe. Submitting an empty query returns all six recipes, which is the intended way to browse the catalogue.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Two consequences follow directly. First, <strong>matching is against names, not pattern contents</strong>. Searching for <code>\\d</code> or <code>#</code> returns nothing, even though digits and hashes appear inside several of the patterns, because those characters are not part of any recipe name. Search by purpose. Second, because the filter is a plain substring test with no fuzzy matching, approximate queries fail rather than degrade — <code>emails</code> matches only if a recipe name contains that exact sequence, so the singular forms are what you want.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Results are returned as a name-and-pattern pair. When nothing matches, the output says so explicitly rather than returning an empty panel, which makes a zero-result search easy to distinguish from a failed one. Because the catalogue is fixed at six entries, a no-match result is a reliable signal that the pattern you need is outside this library rather than a hint to reword your query.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>One practical note: <a href=\"/developers/regex-library\">Regex Library</a> currently exposes the same six recipes through the same lookup, so the two tools return identical results for identical queries. Treat them as one catalogue reached by two routes.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Search", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Enter a query in the input area.</strong> Type the <em>kind of data</em> you are looking for — <code>email</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>ipv4</code>, <code>phone</code>, <code>hex</code> or <code>date</code>. Partial words work, since the test is a substring match.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Or submit empty to browse.</strong> Leaving the input blank returns the complete six-recipe catalogue, which is the fastest way to see whether the library covers what you need before you start typing.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Read the result as a name and pattern pair.</strong> The output pairs the recipe name with its pattern, separated for readability. Copy the pattern body only; the name is a label, not part of the expression.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Test against real samples before trusting it.</strong> Paste the pattern into the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> with input that includes both values that should match and values that should not. Every recipe here is intended for finding, so a pattern can look perfect and still be far too permissive for your use case.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Anchor it if you are validating a field.</strong> None of the recipes is anchored. Wrapping the pattern in <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> converts a finder into something that rejects input with extra characters around a valid value.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Adding a quick validation check.</strong> When you need an email or URL check today and want a reasonable starting point rather than a blank field, a recipe gets you something testable in seconds — provided you harden it before it gates anything.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Scraping and log mining.</strong> Pulling email addresses, URLs or IP addresses out of unstructured text is precisely what these loose patterns are good at, because you want recall rather than precision and you will filter the results afterwards.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Sanitising data before sharing.</strong> Finding candidate phone numbers or email addresses so they can be replaced with placeholders is a common and appropriate use of a permissive finder.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Starting a pattern you will then tighten.</strong> A working baseline that over-matches is easier to restrict than an empty field is to fill, particularly for address and date formats where the failure modes are well known.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Checking a requirement against a reference implementation.</strong> When a ticket specifies ISO dates or hex colours, the recipe shows the conventional shape and gives the reviewer something concrete to compare against.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Suppose you need to find IPv4 addresses in a log excerpt. Enter <code>ipv4</code> and the search returns the recipe <code>\\b(?:\\d{1,3}\\.){3}\\d{1,3}\\b</code>. Reading it: a word boundary, then a non-capturing group repeated exactly three times that matches one to three digits followed by a literal dot, then one to three digits, then a word boundary. The non-capturing group is the right choice here, since the repetition is what matters and you do not want three separate capture groups cluttering the result.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now test it in the Regex Tester against <code>10.0.0.7 and 999.999.999.999</code>. Both are reported as matches. That is the recipe behaving exactly as written — <code>\\d{1,3}</code> constrains how many digits an octet has, not what number it represents — but it is almost certainly not what you want if you are validating addresses rather than finding them.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The fix is to constrain each octet to 0–255. A range-accurate octet is normally written as an alternation over <code>0-199</code>, <code>200-249</code> and <code>250-255</code>, which makes the pattern noticeably longer and harder to read. For most codebases the better answer is to use the recipe for discovery and then split on dots and validate each part numerically — a few lines of ordinary code, easier to review, and impossible to get subtly wrong. That trade-off is the general lesson: regex is excellent at <em>finding</em> structured text and a poor substitute for arithmetic.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Search by purpose, not by symbol.</strong> The filter matches recipe names, so <code>email</code> works and <code>@</code> does not.</li><li><strong>Assume over-matching.</strong> These patterns are finders. The email recipe permits spaces, the phone recipe matches any long digit run, and every one of them matches inside larger strings because none is anchored.</li><li><strong>Anchor before validating.</strong> Add <code>^</code> and <code>$</code> when a pattern is going to decide whether a field is acceptable.</li><li><strong>Do not use the IPv4 recipe as a validator.</strong> It accepts octets above 255. Use it to find addresses, then check the octets numerically.</li><li><strong>Prefer numeric checks to clever regex.</strong> Ranges, calendar validity and check digits are all easier and clearer in ordinary code than in a pattern.</li><li><strong>Do not read the recipes as specifications.</strong> They are pragmatic approximations of formats that are broader and messier than any single pattern captures — which is why strict email validation by regex is a well-known dead end.</li><li><strong>Remember the catalogue is small.</strong> Six recipes is a starting point, not a library. For anything else, build the pattern and verify it in the tester.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why did my search return no results when I searched for a pattern fragment?</h3>Because matching is against recipe <em>names</em>, not pattern contents. Search for the kind of data — <code>email</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>phone</code> — rather than symbols or fragments that appear inside the patterns." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How many patterns are in the library?</h3>Six: email, URL, IPv4 address, phone number, hexadecimal colour and ISO date. Submitting an empty query lists all of them, which is the quickest way to confirm whether the library covers your case." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Can I trust a recipe for form validation?</h3>Not as-is. Every recipe is written to find matches rather than to reject invalid input, so all of them over-match. Anchor the pattern and tighten it for your specific constraints — and for anything involving ranges or arithmetic, validate in code instead of in the pattern." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the email recipe match every valid address?</h3>No. It rejects quoted local parts, which the specification permits, and it accepts some shapes that are not deliverable, including consecutive dots and embedded spaces. Treat it as a rough filter that catches obvious mistakes, and confirm actual addresses by sending mail to them." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How is this different from the Regex Cheatsheet?</h3>The Regex Cheatsheet lists syntax — quantifiers, groups, character classes and their meanings. This tool returns complete patterns for specific kinds of data. One teaches the vocabulary, the other hands you a finished sentence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Is Regex Library a different set of patterns?</h3>No. It exposes the same six recipes through the same lookup, so identical queries return identical results. The pages differ in what they document about the recipes, not in what the tools return." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Run any recipe you take from here through the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> against real samples before it decides anything, and if you need to rewrite matched values rather than find them, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-replace-tester\">Regex Replace Tester</a> applies a substitution and reports the size change. To understand the tokens inside a recipe — the non-capturing groups and bounded quantifiers in the IPv4 pattern, for instance — the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a> is the reference, and the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> will inventory any pattern you paste.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("regex-cheatsheet-search", regex_cheatsheet_searchSeo);

const cron_to_regexSeo: ToolSeo = {
  sections: [
    { heading: "What the Cron Field Decoder Does", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>This tool takes a five-field cron expression and <strong>breaks it back down into its named fields</strong>. You paste something like <code>*/15 9-17 * * 1-5</code> and get back a labelled list: which value belongs to the minute field, which to the hour, which to day-of-month, month and day-of-week.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>One point must be stated plainly, because the tool's name overpromises. It does <strong>not</strong> generate a regular expression. It decodes and labels the fields, and it says so in its own output — the result explicitly notes that cron fields are not regular and that a true regex cannot express rules like <em>last Friday</em>, directing you to use the decoded breakdown to build a pattern for your specific case.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Used that way it is genuinely useful. The most common cron mistakes are not mechanical failures but misinterpretations — reading the day fields in the wrong order, or assuming that restricting day-of-month and day-of-week will intersect when the customary behaviour is to unite them. Having each field named and separated in front of you is an effective guard against both.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "How the Decoding Works", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The implementation is a splitter and a labeller. The input is trimmed and split on runs of whitespace into up to five parts, and each part is printed against its field name in the conventional order.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>If the input contains fewer than five whitespace-separated parts, the tool refuses with a message explaining that a cron expression has five fields — minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week. That guard is the tool's main piece of validation. Anything beyond five fields is not rejected; the split simply produces more parts, and the first five are the ones labelled.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>What the decode does not do is interpret the field contents. It does not expand ranges into the set of values they represent, does not evaluate step syntax, does not tell you what day of the week a given day-of-week number is, and does not check that a value falls inside its field's legal range. A <code>99</code> in the minute field is labelled as a minute and passed through without complaint, even though no cron implementation would accept it. The output is a structural breakdown, and validating the numbers is your job.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The refusal to emit a regex is a correctness decision rather than a limitation to be worked around. Cron schedules express <em>ordinal and positional</em> constraints — the third Friday of the month, the last weekday before a given date — which depend on counting within a span whose length varies. Deterministic finite automata cannot count, so a general translation is not possible. It is true that for any <em>fixed</em> calendar range you could construct a regex that matches exactly the timestamps that range produces, but such a pattern would be enormous, tied to specific years, and useless the moment the range moved.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Using the Decoder", content: [
      { type: "list", items: [
        "<p><strong>Step 1 — Paste the cron expression into the input area.</strong> Include all five fields, separated by spaces. Cron tolerates multiple spaces, and so does this tool, since it splits on runs of whitespace.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 2 — Run the transform.</strong> There are no options, flags or timezone settings. The output is purely a field-by-field reading of the text you supplied.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 3 — Check the field count.</strong> A message saying a cron expression has five fields means your input was too short — often because the expression was truncated on copy or because you pasted only the time-related fields.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 4 — Read the labelled breakdown against the field meanings.</strong> The output names each field in order and prints its value, then repeats the caveat about regularity. Use it to confirm that the value you meant as the day-of-week is in the day-of-week position and not the day-of-month position.</p>",
        "<p><strong>Step 5 — Validate the values yourself.</strong> The decode does not range-check. Confirm minutes fall in 0–59, hours in 0–23, day-of-month in 1–31, month in 1–12, and day-of-week in 0–7 — and decide explicitly whether <code>0</code> or <code>7</code> is Sunday in your scheduler, because implementations vary.</p>",
      ]},
    ]},
    { heading: "Common Use Cases", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Reviewing a schedule before it goes live.</strong> Job schedules are configured once and then left alone, which is exactly the situation where a misplaced field becomes a silent five-minute-interval job instead of a nightly one. Reading the labelled fields back is a cheap check before committing.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Debugging a job that runs at the wrong time.</strong> When a task fires more or less often than expected, the first question is which field holds the value you intended. The decode answers that structurally, without requiring you to recall the field order from memory.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><strong>Translating a schedule between systems.</strong> Moving from a traditional five-field scheduler into a Kubernetes CronJob or a CI pipeline raises questions about field count, about whether seconds are supported, and about which day-of-week numbering applies. Seeing the five labelled fields is the starting point for reconciling those differences.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Worked Example", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Paste a realistic weekday schedule:</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p><code>*/15 9-17 * * 1-5</code></p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>The decode echoes the expression and labels the five fields: minute <code>*/15</code>, hour <code>9-17</code>, day-of-month <code>*</code>, month <code>*</code>, day-of-week <code>1-5</code>. It then restates that cron fields are not regular and suggests using this breakdown to construct a pattern for your own case.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Reading those fields is where the value lies. The minute field uses a step: every fifteen minutes within the hour, so at :00, :15, :30 and :45. The hour field is a range from 9 to 17 inclusive — and note that this includes <code>17</code>, so the final run of the day starts at 17:45 rather than 17:00, which is a common off-by-one-hour surprise. Both day fields are unrestricted, meaning every date qualifies. The day-of-week range <code>1-5</code> selects Monday through Friday under the customary numbering where <code>0</code> is Sunday.</p>" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>Now the trap. Change the expression to <code>0 0 1 * 1</code> — the first of the month, and Mondays. Almost everyone reads that as <em>the first of the month only when it is a Monday</em>. In the customary cron behaviour the two day fields are combined with <strong>or</strong>, not <strong>and</strong>: the job runs on the first of every month, and on every Monday, because whenever both day fields are restricted each is treated as a separate trigger condition. If you genuinely need the intersection, you have to express it in your application logic rather than in the schedule. The decoder will not warn you about this, which is why the field meanings are worth knowing rather than just recognising.</p>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Tips and Common Mistakes", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<ul><li><strong>Remember the field order.</strong> Minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week. Reversing the last two is the single most common scheduling error, and the labelled output is designed to make that reversal obvious.</li><li><strong>Watch the day-of-month and day-of-week <em>or</em> rule.</strong> When both are restricted, conventional cron runs the job when <em>either</em> matches. If you need both, use a wider schedule and gate it in code.</li><li><strong>Know your day-of-week numbering.</strong> <code>0</code> is usually Sunday with <code>7</code> accepted as an alias, but some schedulers use <code>1</code> for Sunday. Confirm against your own platform before shipping.</li><li><strong>Count the fields.</strong> Five is standard for traditional cron. Some implementations accept six with a seconds field or a leading year, and the boundary between the two conventions is a frequent source of jobs that fire far too often.</li><li><strong>Step values include both ends.</strong> <code>9-17</code> with a step of one includes 17. Ranges are inclusive, so the last run of the day is later than intuition suggests.</li><li><strong>Schedules are timezone-dependent.</strong> This tool decodes fields and says nothing about timezones. An expression means different wall-clock times depending on the server's timezone, and daylight-saving transitions can cause a job to run twice or not at all.</li><li><strong>Check whether seconds are supported before using a sixth field.</strong> Some schedulers interpret six fields as seconds-first rather than minutes-first, which silently shifts every part of the schedule.</li><li><strong>Validate the numbers separately.</strong> The decoder does not range-check fields, so an out-of-range value is labelled and passed through rather than flagged.</li></ul>" },
    ]},
    { heading: "Frequently Asked Questions", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does this tool convert a cron expression into a regular expression?</h3>No. It decodes the expression into its named fields and states that cron fields are not regular. The name is misleading on this point. A general conversion is not possible, because constraints such as <em>the third Friday</em> require counting within spans of varying length, which finite automata cannot do." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Why do I get a message about needing five fields?</h3>Because fewer than five whitespace-separated parts were supplied. The expression was probably truncated when copied, or only the time fields were pasted. Check the source and paste the complete expression." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>Does the decoder expand ranges and steps?</h3>No. It labels each field and prints its value as written. It does not expand <code>9-17</code> into twelve hours, does not evaluate <code>*/15</code>, and does not verify that anything falls inside its legal range." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<h3>How do I check whether my expression is actually valid?</h3>Compare each decoded field against its documented range — minutes 0–59, hours 0–23, day-of-month 1–31, month 1–12, day-of-week 0–7 — and then confirm against the scheduler you are actually targeting, since implementations differ on special characters and numbering. This tool deliberately does not validate." },
    ]},
    { heading: "Related Tools", content: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "<p>If you need to match timestamp strings rather than schedule them, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet-search\">Regex Cheatsheet Search</a> returns an ISO date recipe and the <a href=\"/developers/regex-tester\">Regex Tester</a> will confirm what it matches and captures. To understand the tokens in any pattern you build from this breakdown, the <a href=\"/developers/regex-cheatsheet\">Regex Cheatsheet</a> covers the syntax, and the <a href=\"/developers/regex-explainer\">Regex Explainer</a> inventories the constructs present in a pattern you paste.</p>" },
    ]},
    PRIVACY_SECURITY_BLOCK,
  ],
};
registerToolSeo("cron-to-regex", cron_to_regexSeo);

